{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && sed -n '270,305p' <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md && sed -n '2218,2235p' <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n### rollout_summary_files\n\n- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T19-10-40-cRYC-standalone_lean_stabilizer_hyperfinite_formalization_blocked.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T21-10-40-019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T03:04:54+00:00, thread_id=019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2, partial: algebraic results checked; full theorem blocked)\n\n### keywords\n\n- StarAlgEquiv, isUnitalTracial_pullback, CStarEquivExtension.lean, CoherentPhaseQubitState.lean, AlgebraicLimitTraceTransport.lean, UHF, GNS, II_∞, hyperfinite-II1\n\n## Task 2: preserve the full C*- and von Neumann theorem boundary\n\n### rollout_summary_files\n\n- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T19-10-40-cRYC-standalone_lean_stabilizer_hyperfinite_formalization_blocked.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T21-10-40-019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T03:04:54+00:00, thread_id=019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2, blocked: source and library lack required analytic inputs)\n\n### keywords\n\n- gLU, refinement maps, Pauli multiplier, C*-completion, product-state factorization, quasi-equivalence, bicommutant, normal trace, hyperfinite factor classification, PROGRESS.md\n\n## User preferences\n\n- when the user asked for “a new folder, not in the overleaf repo” and requested an explanation before starting -> preserve the separate project boundary and approval-gated setup [Task 1]\n- when the user said the existing proof may contain errors -> treat manuscript claims as hypotheses to audit, not authoritative facts [Task 1]\n- when the user requested a tracial state induced by the tensor product -> track state existence separately from algebraic isomorphism and factor classification [Task 1]\n\n## Reusable knowledge\n\n- `B(H) \\bar⊗ R` has a tracial state only for finite-dimensional `H`; for infinite-dimensional `H`, its relevant trace is faithful normal semifinite and the factor is `II_∞`. [Task 1]\n- Checked modules: `Formalization/TraceTransport.lean` proves `StabilizerHyperfinite.isUnitalTracial_pullback`; `CStarEquivExtension.lean` establishes continuity/isometry only for supplied complex C*-algebra `StarAlgEquiv`s; `AlgebraicLimitStarHom.lean`/`AlgebraicLimitTraceTransport.lean` are algebraic direct limits; `CoherentPhaseQubitState.lean` proves the selected-Z phase-weighted expectation from coherent finite-stage data. [Task 1]\n- `lake build`, focused module builds, axiom audits, and two-pass `pdflatex` for `blueprints/001-tensor-product-trace.tex` passed. There were no `sorry`, `admit`, or user axioms in checked sources. [Task 1]\n- Missing inputs remain coherent finite-stage gLU/refinement maps, Pauli multiplier/involution and phase calibration, quasi-local C*-completion, product-state factorization, normal/spatial GNS-bicommutant identification, and hyperfinite-factor classification. [Task 2]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- symptom: a short-range manuscript argument is promoted to the full regional theorem -> cause: coherent-coordinate and analytic hypotheses are missing -> fix: state the result conditionally or retain the analytic obligations explicitly. [Task 2]\n- symptom: Lean axioms are used to mask completion/normality/classification gaps -> fix: do not hide them; resume only with concrete gLU/refinement data, approval for a strengthened conditional theorem, or a deliberate infrastructure project. [Task 2]\n\n- Lean, mathlib, CStarAlgebra, WStarAlgebra, VonNeumannAlgebra, GNS, PositiveLinearMap.GNS, Pauli, partial-trace, support-corner, Kaplansky-density, ultraweak, semifinite, technical.tex\n\n## User preferences\n\n- when the user said \"No QIQT\" -> restrict formalization claims to upstream mathlib and separate completed theorems from interfaces and TODOs [Task 1]\n- for this stabilizer project, retain `G_F=G\\cap A_F` and broad proof-compatible conditional framing; do not turn a semifiniteness certificate into factoriality or type-`II_\\infty` classification [Task 1]\n\n## Reusable knowledge\n\n- A realistic first Lean layer is finite qubit matrix/tensor products, Pauli trace orthogonality, finite abelian stabilizer groups, group-average `P_F`, rank/density formula, and partial-trace compatibility. Canonical-state existence/positivity may be assumed initially. [Task 1]\n- Upstream mathlib has C*-algebra classes, spectra, continuous functional calculus, positivity/CP maps, and GNS (`PositiveLinearMap.GNS`, `gnsStarAlgHom`, `gnsNonUnitalStarAlgHom`); it lacks ready-made Pauli/stabilizer/partial-trace libraries. [Task 1]\n- Treat the support-corner-to-semifiniteness argument as a separate frontier: strong/strong-* topology, bicommutants, Kaplansky density, ultraweak normality, finite projections/central support, and full-finite-projection-implies-semifinite are not an integrated finished package. Defer central direct-integral/factor classification. Suggested staged interfaces: `finite_stabilizer_density_and_partial_trace`, `support_monotonicity_from_compatible_projector_states`, `abstract_support_corner_is_tracial`, `full_finite_projection_implies_semifinite`. [Task 1]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- symptom: tooling/path claims are based on a presumed checkout -> cause: invalid `functions.exec_command` call and relative instruction paths -> fix: use `tools.exec_command` and inspect confirmed target paths before relying on project files. [Task 1]\n\n# Task Group: Thunderbird reply-thread to ODS registration-row handoff\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
