{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && printf '\\\\n--- root files ---\\\\n' && for f in AGENTS.md PLAN.md work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md aristotle_tasks/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.md SOURCE-CONTRACT.md CONTEXT-LEDGER.md PROGRESS.md; do if [ -f \\\"\"'$f\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n### %s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; fi; done && printf '\"'\\\\n--- review/handoff listing ---\\\\n' && rg --files reviews work AristotleCandidates aristotle_tasks 2>/dev/null | sort | tail -180\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && printf '\\\\n--- root files ---\\\\n' && for f in AGENTS.md PLAN.md work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md aristotle_tasks/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.md SOURCE-CONTRACT.md CONTEXT-LEDGER.md PROGRESS.md; do if [ -f \\\"\"'$f\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n### %s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; fi; done && printf '\"'\\\\n--- review/handoff listing ---\\\\n' && rg --files reviews work AristotleCandidates aristotle_tasks 2>/dev/null | sort | tail -180\"","aggregated_output":"# Aristotle CLI reference\n\n## Setup\n\n- Install: `uv tool install aristotlelib`\n- Verify: `aristotle --version`\n- Authenticate: set `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` in the shell environment, or use the skill wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\nIf the key is stored near the end of `~/.bashrc`, noninteractive commands may not see it because many `.bashrc` files contain:\n\n```bash\ncase $- in\n    *i*) ;;\n      *) return;;\nesac\n```\n\nIn that case, do not rely on `source ~/.bashrc`. Use the wrapper, which reads a literal `export ARISTOTLE_API_KEY=...` assignment without printing it:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize input.md\n```\n\n## Core commands\n\nSubmit a Lean project for sorry filling or proof search:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh submit \"Fill in all sorries\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\nFormalize a TeX or paper source:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize paper.tex --wait --destination output.tar.gz\n```\n\nList recent jobs:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh list --limit 10\n```\n\nDownload a completed job:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh download <project-id> --destination result.tar.gz\n```\n\nPass the key directly for one command only:\n\n```bash\naristotle submit \"My prompt\" --api-key \"$ARISTOTLE_API_KEY\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\n## Recommended project loop\n\n1. Inspect the Lean project locally.\n2. Run the narrowest local build or test that exercises the target files.\n3. Submit a focused Aristotle prompt against the project directory.\n4. Wait for the run if the expected result is a concrete patch.\n5. Download or inspect the produced artifact.\n6. Rebuild locally and repeat with a tighter prompt if needed.\n\n## Prompt shape\n\nKeep prompts short and file-bound. Good prompts mention:\n\n- the exact project directory\n- the theorem or file to work on\n- whether to fill `sorry`s, formalize a statement, or debug a failing proof\n- what counts as a useful partial result\n\nAvoid vague prompts such as \"improve this project\" unless the scope is intentionally broad.\n\n--- root files ---\n\n### AGENTS.md\n# Manuscript-Only Context Policy\n\n## Source authority\n\nFor manuscript-facing statements, the sole mathematical source is\n`sources/manuscript/main.tex`.\nThe local summary and locator record is `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`. Do not modify\nthe manuscript from this project.\n\nEvery claim described as coming from the manuscript must identify an exact\nline range in `main.tex`. Repository history, earlier chat context, and other\nartifacts are not substitute sources.\n\n## Context firewall\n\nDo not introduce, assume, or attribute to the manuscript any gLU,\nrefinement, layer-coherence, or finite-coordinate construction unless it is\npresent in the cited manuscript lines or is explicitly introduced as a new\nauxiliary assumption. A Lean file compiling does not make its unstated\nphysical or operator-algebraic hypotheses part of the manuscript.\n\nClassify each item as one of:\n\n1. **manuscript-derived**: exact source locator and no stronger conclusion;\n2. **auxiliary assumption**: explicitly labelled and used only conditionally;\n3. **Lean infrastructure**: reusable formal mathematics that makes no source\n   claim; or\n4. **legacy exploratory work**: retained for reference but unavailable as\n   support for a manuscript-derived assertion.\n\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md` records the current legacy items.\n\n## Current formalization boundary\n\nThe target discussed in the manuscript is the short-range regional-algebra\nstatement at `main.tex:1740-1752`; its proposed proof is at\n`main.tex:1891-2004`. Keep separate the asserted algebraic identification,\nthe product-state/GNS steps, the unformalized operator-algebraic completion and\nspatial tensor-product steps, and the trace conclusion. A tracial state is\nappropriate only for the finite pure-sector case. The infinite pure-sector\ntarget instead calls for a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight when the\nrequired hypotheses and von Neumann infrastructure are supplied.\n\n## Aristotle task packets\n\nEvery new task under `aristotle_tasks/` must begin with exactly one context\ndeclaration:\n\n- `Source basis: main.tex:<line range>` for a manuscript-facing task; or\n- `Infrastructure only:` for reusable Lean material that makes no manuscript\n  claim.\n\nIt must then list every auxiliary assumption, state its exact Lean target, and\nsay whether its conclusion is conditional. A task may not cite a legacy\nartifact as its source basis. Use `aristotle_tasks/TEMPLATE.md` for new\npackets.\n\n## Active two-lane plan execution\n\nThe active Codex/Aristotle execution plan is `PLAN.md`, with the approval\nrecord in `work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md`. Before starting any new\npackage in the P47--P61 program, read both files and the closeout/audit record\nfor every declared predecessor.\n\nEvery future packet must include a `Plan alignment` section containing:\n\n1. the exact package identifier and its lane: `G` (general finite-corner and\n   central type-III exclusion), `H` (explicit local-hyperbolic tensor-factor\n   model), `shared`, or `reconnection`;\n2. the predecessor packages that have already been integrated, or `none` for\n   a foundation package;\n3. the exact gate this package is permitted to discharge; and\n4. downstream claims it is forbidden to make.\n\nThe Aristotle prompt must explicitly instruct the worker to read the active\nplan and respect this section. A package may use only integrated, compiled\npredecessors; a finished remote job is not a dependency until it has passed\nthe local semantic audit. Do not infer a theorem from a later package, and do\nnot submit a dependent packet before its listed prerequisites are integrated.\n\nFor Lane G, the required conclusion is a nonzero finite **projection** in\nevery nonzero central summand; a finite-dimensional subalgebra is not enough.\nFor Lane H, do not call the local algebra a factor until the spatial tensor\nfactorization and factorhood of both factors are proved. Neither lane may\nclaim a global trace/weight unless it constructs or explicitly assumes one.\n\n## Verification and reporting\n\nReport Lean compilation separately from mathematical validation. For each\nmanuscript-facing milestone, state which source clauses are represented, which\nassumptions remain external, and which analytic/von Neumann-algebraic claims\nare not yet formalized in the pinned Mathlib version.\n\n### PLAN.md\n# Active plan: two-lane stabilizer-algebra formalization\n\n## Decision — 2026-08-07\n\nThe project now has two parallel mathematical targets sharing finite\nstabilizer and operator-algebra infrastructure, but neither is allowed to\nsilently supply the other one's hypotheses.\n\n### Lane G — general lattices and central type-III exclusion\n\n**Source basis:** `main.tex:332-342`, with the corner proof at\n`main.tex:1026-1536`.\n\nFor a general consistent stabilizer system and region, construct the decreasing\nlocal-code projection tower, its strong limit `q`, and the finite corner\n`q M_Gamma q`. Prove that the vector functional `tau_q` is a faithful normal\ntracial **state** on that corner, hence that `q` is finite; prove `q` is full;\nand derive that every nonzero central projection `z` has `zq != 0` finite in\n`z M_Gamma`. Conclude that no nonzero central summand is type III.\n\nThis lane does **not** require a direct-integral formalization or a global\ntrace/weight on `M_Gamma`. A global faithful normal semifinite weight is a\nseparate optional strengthening. The condition to formalize is the existence\nof a nonzero finite **projection/corner** in each nonzero central summand, not\nmerely a finite-dimensional subalgebra.\n\n### Lane H — explicit local-hyperbolic tensor-factor model\n\n**Source basis:** `main.tex:1671-1679`, `main.tex:1898-2004`.\n\nFrom a coherent local hyperbolic basis, construct the finite coordinate maps,\ntheir coherent algebraic direct-limit star equivalence, concrete C-star\nmodels, the state identity, and the product GNS tensor splitting. Then prove\nthe special regional algebra is spatially isomorphic to\n\n`B(K_pure) barTensor R_mixed`.\n\nAfter proving the two factors and their spatial tensor product, derive the\ntrivial-center/factor conclusion for this special case. This conclusion does\nnot follow merely from the word \"hyperbolic\"; it follows from the constructed\nspatial factorization.\n\n### Reconnection\n\nLane H gives an explicit factor theorem and, in the infinite-pure case, the\nconcrete faithful normal semifinite weight `Tr barTensor tau`. Lane G gives\nthe general no-central-type-III theorem. A final comparison theorem may show\nthat the Lane-H factor model satisfies the Lane-G finite-corner criterion, but\nthe two results remain logically independent.\n\n## Aristotle package sequence\n\n### Shared/central core\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose and acceptance gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| P47 | `CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean` | Define center, central projections, finite projections, corners, central support, and fullness for the concrete von Neumann-algebra model. Prove only algebraic order/corner lemmas; no direct integral. |\n| P48 | `GeneralLatticeProjectionTower.lean` | Formalize the general-lattice exhaustion, local code projections, monotonicity, and the strong-limit projection `q`. If the stabilizer definitions do not imply a required limit fact, expose it as an auxiliary hypothesis. |\n| P49 | `FiniteCornerTrace.lean` | Derive finite-stage trace cyclicity and pass it to the compressed quasi-local C-star algebra. Keep finite codespace density data explicit. |\n| P49A | `StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann.lean` | Develop the strong-operator convergence/closure API needed for concrete operator algebras; prove commutants and hence concrete von Neumann algebras are strongly closed. This is a proved infrastructure theorem, not an interface. |\n| P49B | `VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean` | Prove the analytic double-commutant theorem for a unital complex star subalgebra of bounded operators: its bicommutant is its strong-operator closure. Develop the required finite amplification/cyclic-subspace machinery locally when Mathlib lacks it. |\n| P49C | `KaplanskyDensity.lean` | Prove bounded/unit-ball Kaplansky density for the represented C-star algebra in its bicommutant and derive the compressed-corner approximation used by the manuscript. No supplied density interface is accepted. |\n| P50 | `NormalFaithfulCornerTrace.lean` | Using P49 and P49C, prove traciality of `tau_q` on `q M_Gamma q` by bounded strong approximation, then prove normality and faithfulness from the exact vector-functional and density/commutation hypotheses used in the manuscript. |\n| P51 | `FullFiniteCornerCentralExclusion.lean` | Prove `q` finite, prove fullness, and derive `0 != z ->` a nonzero finite projection in `z M_Gamma`; define/use the project-local type-III obstruction. |\n\n### Explicit local-hyperbolic lane\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose and acceptance gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| P52 | `CoherentLocalHyperbolicCoordinates.lean` | Build finite coordinate blocks and an exhaustion from local hyperbolic data. Audit whether finite support suffices; otherwise isolate the exact local-finiteness/coherence assumption. |\n| P53 | `LocalHyperbolicAlgebraicAlpha.lean` | Construct the finite stage maps, phase calibration, and coherent algebraic `alpha`; instantiate the existing direct-limit theorems without supplied stage equivalences. |\n| P54 | `UHFReferenceRepresentation.lean` | Construct a faithful compatible Hilbert/operator representation of the dyadic algebra from finite stages; do not assume an infinite tensor-product Hilbert space. |\n| P55 | `RegionalConcreteCStarAlpha.lean` | Construct source and regional closed operator-range C-star algebras and extend the P53 alpha by density. |\n| P56 | `RegionalProductStateIdentity.lean` | Prove the stabilizer/product-state equality on finite Pauli words and extend it by density and continuity. |\n| P57 | `ProductGNSTensorFactorization.lean` | Construct the pure/mixed product GNS Hilbert-space tensor splitting and the spatial represented-algebra identification. |\n| P58 | `MixedUHFHyperfiniteFactor.lean` | Construct/prove the finite mixed matrix and infinite mixed hyperfinite-II1 alternatives under project-local factor and normal-trace definitions. |\n| P59 | `LocalHyperbolicFactorTraceTheorem.lean` | Transport P57--P58 to the regional algebra, prove trivial center, and state the finite-pure trace versus infinite-pure faithful-normal-semifinite-weight dichotomy. |\n\n### Optional strengthening/reconnection\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| P60 | `FullCornerSemifiniteWeight.lean` | Optional globalization of a full finite corner to a faithful normal semifinite weight, if needed beyond no-type-III exclusion. |\n| P61 | `HyperbolicImpliesCentralCriterion.lean` | Show the explicit Lane-H model supplies the finite-corner criterion used by Lane G. |\n\n## Initial parallel wave\n\nSubmit only the four independent foundation packets first: P47, P48, P52,\nand P54. Integrate and semantically audit them before submitting dependent\npackets. Existing P42--P46 task drafts are preserved as historical drafts;\nthey are not evidence and must be re-scoped against this plan before reuse.\n\n## Non-negotiable gates\n\n1. No claim that `q M_Gamma q` has merely a semifinite weight when the\n   construction actually targets a finite faithful normal tracial state.\n2. No claim of a global trace/weight on `M_Gamma` from the finite-corner\n   theorem alone.\n3. No claim that a finite-dimensional subalgebra excludes type III; the proof\n   must produce a nonzero finite projection in every nonzero central summand.\n4. No claim that hyperbolic data imply a factor before the spatial tensor\n   factorization and factorhood of both factors are proved.\n5. Every manuscript-facing packet identifies an exact `main.tex` locator and\n   every extra analytic hypothesis appears as a named field, never an axiom.\n6. P49A--P49C are mandatory proved infrastructure. P50 may not replace the\n   bicommutant theorem or Kaplansky density by a supplied structure field; an\n   incomplete analytic package halts Lane G and triggers review.\n\n---\n\n# Previous single-lane plan: retained background\n\n## Objective\n\nFormalize, from the manuscript's actual finite-support adapted-coordinate data,\na state-preserving C-star isomorphism from the quasi-local Pauli algebra to the\nregional algebra.  Obtain the represented von Neumann algebra equivalence by\nthe canonical GNS unitary, then apply an independently established product\nfactor classification.\n\nThe source basis is exclusively `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md` and the cited manuscript\nlocators.  In particular, no gLU/refinement/layer-coherence premise is allowed\nunless later added explicitly as a new auxiliary assumption.\n\n## Current position\n\nThe project already has checked conditional interfaces for:\n\n1. finite Pauli/stabilizer coordinates and pure/mixed states;\n2. algebraic direct limits, their induced algebraic equivalence `alpha`, and\n   compatible state transport;\n3. supplied C-star completions and a supplied C-star extension of `alpha`;\n4. GNS transport for supplied compatible C-star states; and\n5. supplied normal/product-factor transport.\n\nThese are scaffolding.  They do not discharge the manuscript-specific finite\ncoordinate construction or the product-factor classification.\n\n## Phase A — exact finite coordinate theorem\n\n**Goal:** Replace abstract finite-stage star equivalences by maps constructed\nfrom a finite restriction of the adapted symplectic basis.\n\n1. Specify a finite region/exhaustion and the finite Pauli label spaces.\n2. Define the coordinate map from the adapted `z_i,x_i` data.\n3. Prove the Pauli multiplier and star equations, including the required phase\n   correction.\n4. Prove the map is a finite-stage complex star-algebra equivalence.\n5. Prove its stabilizer membership criterion and the finite product-state\n   expectation formula.\n\n**Gate A:** no supplied finite-stage star equivalence remains in the finite\nmanuscript theorem; only explicitly stated finite adapted-coordinate data does.\n\n## Phase B — coherence and quasi-local C-star isomorphism\n\n**Goal:** Construct the actual C-star isomorphism `alpha`, rather than assume\nan algebraic or completed extension.\n\n1. Prove the Phase-A maps commute exactly with all finite-region inclusions.\n2. Form the algebraic direct-limit star equivalence.\n3. Specify the quasi-local C-star completions as inductive-limit completions.\n4. Prove the algebraic map is isometric on the dense quasi-local subalgebras.\n5. Extend it uniquely to a C-star star-algebra equivalence\n   `alpha : P_quasilocal ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ] A_regional`.\n\n**Gate B:** P23 and P25 are instantiated by proved data, not used as supplied\ninterfaces.\n\n## Phase C — state identity and GNS transport\n\n**Goal:** Prove the regional state pulled back by `alpha` is the pure/mixed\nproduct state.\n\n1. Check equality on finite Pauli-word bases using Phase A's stabilizer\ncriterion.\n2. Prove compatibility under inclusions and hence equality on the quasi-local\nC-star algebra by density/continuity.\n3. Instantiate the existing C-star/GNS bridge to obtain the canonical GNS\nunitary and its intertwining identity.\n4. Use conjugation by that unitary to identify the represented von Neumann\nalgebras.\n\n**Gate C:** do not use a separate quasi-equivalence/normal-folium argument.\nIt is unnecessary once the state-preserving C-star isomorphism is proved.\n\n## Phase D — product representation classification\n\n**Goal:** Independently prove or source the product-state representation\ntheorem.\n\n1. Split coordinate indices into pure and mixed sectors.\n2. Establish the infinite-product GNS factorization.\n3. Identify the pure sector as `B(K_pure)`.\n4. Identify the mixed sector as a finite matrix factor when finite, or the\nhyperfinite `II_1` factor when infinite.\n5. Establish the spatial tensor-product identification of the product\nrepresentation.\n\n**Gate D:** every analytic theorem is either formalized against a supported\nMathlib API or recorded as an exact external theorem with source locator and\nexplicit hypothesis.\n\n## Phase E — theorem and trace corollaries\n\n1. Transport the Phase-D product classification through the Phase-C GNS\nunitary to the regional von Neumann algebra.\n2. State the finite-pure and infinite-pure cases separately.\n3. Finite pure sector: derive the normalized tracial-state conclusion.\n4. Infinite pure sector: state the faithful normal semifinite trace/weight\nconclusion; do not call it a tracial state.\n5. Audit every conclusion against `main.tex:1740-1752` and record any required\nmanuscript correction separately.\n\n## Parallelization\n\nUntil Phase B is blocked on a single coherent-coordinate construction, run in\nparallel:\n\n- finite phase/cocycle verification;\n- finite adapted-coordinate/stabilizer-state verification;\n- finite inclusion/coherence lemmas;\n- Mathlib/API feasibility audits for C-star inductive limits and GNS products;\n- product mixed-sector classification/source work.\n\nDo not parallelize mutually dependent attempts at the same global `alpha`.\nEach new Aristotle packet must use `aristotle_tasks/TEMPLATE.md` and state its\nsource basis plus every auxiliary assumption.\n\n## Exit criterion\n\nCall the manuscript theorem formally established only when Gates A--D are\ndischarged and Phase E compiles without substituting supplied interfaces for\nthe central finite-coordinate, C-star-extension, state-identity, or\nproduct-classification claims.\n\n### work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md\n# Two-lane formalization decision\n\nDate: 2026-08-07\n\nThe active Codex/Aristotle loop follows the two-lane plan in `PLAN.md`.\n\n**Lane G** formalizes the manuscript's general-lattice corner argument:\nthe strong-limit code projection `q`, the faithful normal finite tracial state\non `q M_Gamma q`, finiteness and fullness of `q`, and the conclusion that each\nnonzero central summand has a nonzero finite projection and hence is not type\nIII. This route does not use a direct integral and does not require a global\ntrace/weight on `M_Gamma`.\n\n**Lane H** formalizes the explicit short-range/local-hyperbolic model:\ncoherent coordinate maps, an algebraic and C-star `alpha`, the product-state\nidentity, product GNS tensor factorization, and the resulting factor and\ntrace/weight classification.\n\nThe lanes meet only after their own gates are met. In particular, the explicit\ntensor-factor result proves trivial center in the special local-hyperbolic\ncase, whereas the general corner result rules out type-III central summands\nwithout asserting trivial center.\n\nThe first approved parallel Aristotle wave is P47, P48, P52, and P54. No\ndependent packet should be submitted before its prerequisite results are\nintegrated and semantically audited.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-07: the analytic passage from the compressed quasi-local\nC-star algebra to the von Neumann corner is mandatory project infrastructure.\nAfter P48, the loop must prove strong closedness of concrete von Neumann\nalgebras (P49A), the analytic double-commutant theorem (P49B), and Kaplansky\nunit-ball density with its compressed-corner corollary (P49C). P50 depends on\nboth the finite-stage/C-star trace P49 and the proved P49C result. These core\nanalytic theorems may require new local topology machinery, but may not be\nreplaced by axioms or supplied interfaces.\n\n### aristotle_tasks/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.md\n# P49B — analytic von Neumann bicommutant theorem\n\n## Context declaration\n\n- **Source basis:** `main.tex:1269-1287`.\n\n## Plan alignment\n\n- **Package and lane:** `P49B`; Lane `shared` analytic infrastructure used by G.\n- **Predecessors:** `P49A`, integrated and locally audited before submission.\n- **Gate discharged:** prove that the bicommutant of a represented unital\n  complex star algebra is its strong-operator closure.\n- **Forbidden downstream claims:** no Kaplansky unit-ball density, no corner\n  trace, no normality/faithfulness conclusion, no finiteness/fullness, and no\n  factor classification.\n\nRead `PLAN.md` and `work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md` before editing.\n\n## Mathematical target\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean`. For a\nunital complex star subalgebra `A ⊆ B(H)`, prove both inclusions and the equality\n\n`strongOperatorClosure A = A''`.\n\nDevelop the missing proof locally. A permissible route is the standard\nfinite-amplification argument: simultaneous approximation on finitely many\nvectors, the cyclic subspace generated by the diagonal amplification of `A`,\nits orthogonal projection in the amplified commutant, and invariance under an\noperator in `A''`. The theorem must cover arbitrary finite strong neighborhoods,\nnot merely sequential closure or one-vector approximation.\n\n## Auxiliary assumptions\n\nState the exact unitality/nondegeneracy hypotheses genuinely used. The central\nequality must be proved from them. It may not be represented by a structure\nfield, postulate, external-theorem token, or new axiom.\n\n## Files and imports\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean`. Import P49A\nand the algebraic bicommutant core. Necessary finite direct-sum/amplification\nsupporting definitions may live in this owned module. Do not import P49C or P50.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem` succeeds.\n- The strong-closure/bicommutant equality is an actual Lean theorem and handles\n  nets/finite strong neighborhoods, not only sequences.\n- No forbidden declaration or supplied replacement of the theorem occurs.\n- If more local topology is required, develop it in the owned module. If the\n  theorem remains unproved, halt and report the minimal remaining lemma graph.\n\n### SOURCE-CONTRACT.md\n# Manuscript source contract\n\n## Authoritative source\n\n- Manuscript: `sources/manuscript/main.tex`\n- Title in source: *Infinite Magic and the Continuum* (`main.tex:117`)\n- Formalization policy: `AGENTS.md`\n\nThis project does not treat its own Lean files, prior agent prompts, or the\nsurrounding repository as mathematical source authority.\n\n## Source clauses in scope\n\n| Source locator | What the manuscript says | Formalization status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `main.tex:1671-1679` | Defines short-range order through a local symplectic basis, with `W_Gamma = span{z_i : i in P}` and finite support. | Requires a precise Lean model of the infinite Pauli/symplectic data. |\n| `main.tex:1740-1752` | States the short-range regional-algebra theorem, with an asserted factorization `A_Gamma` into a pure-sector type-I factor and a core factor that is finite type I or hyperfinite `II_1`. | Target statement; not established by current Lean artifacts. |\n| `main.tex:1915-1920` | Proposes an adapted symplectic basis and a star-isomorphism from an infinite Pauli algebra to `A_Gamma`. | External assumption/gap until the source construction is formalized. |\n| `main.tex:1927-1931` | Writes a pure/mixed product density state. | Finite density-matrix fragments are formalized; the infinite model is not. |\n| `main.tex:1945-1967` | Proposes the GNS/tensor-factor and type conclusions. | Conditional GNS transport is formalized; analytic product and type conclusions are not. |\n| `main.tex:1970-2004` | Proposes quasi-equivalence and normal-folium transport. | Not formalized. |\n\nThe wording above is a locator-oriented paraphrase, not a replacement for the\nmanuscript.\n\n## Permitted auxiliary assumptions\n\nAny manuscript-facing Lean theorem that needs one of the following must expose\nit in the theorem statement or task packet:\n\n1. a completed C-star or von Neumann algebra model for the infinite Pauli\n   system and its regional algebra;\n2. the asserted star-isomorphism from that model to the regional algebra;\n3. the product-state formula and hypotheses making the GNS representation a\n   spatial tensor product;\n4. the von Neumann closure, normality, and quasi-equivalence assumptions needed\n   to transport the claimed factor type; and\n5. any hyperfiniteness or trace theorem used for the core factor.\n\nNone of these assumptions is supplied merely by the phrase “short-range\norder” in the current manuscript source.\n\n## Trace correction\n\nThe manuscript-facing target must distinguish two cases:\n\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is finite-dimensional, a normalized tracial\n  state can be obtained from the finite matrix trace together with the core\n  trace;\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional, the appropriate\n  conclusion is a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight on the `II_infinity`\n  target, not a tracial state.\n\nThis is a correction to the intended theorem contract, not a claim that the\ncurrent manuscript already states or proves the corrected dichotomy.\n\n## Legacy firewall\n\nEarlier gLU/refinement/layer-coherence material is listed in\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`. It is retained, but it is neither a source clause nor an\nimplicit assumption of this formalization.\n\n### CONTEXT-LEDGER.md\n# Context ledger\n\n## Legacy exploratory material: not manuscript authority\n\nThe following artifacts contain gLU, refinement, or layer-coherence language\nimported from broader repository history rather than from the manuscript named\nin `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`:\n\n- `blueprints/002-aristotle-parallel-formalization.tex`;\n- `aristotle_tasks/P6-layered-direct-limit.md`;\n- `AristotleCandidates/LayeredDirectLimit.lean`;\n- `AristotleCandidates/RegionalWitness.lean`; and\n- `Formalization/CoherentPhaseQubitState.lean`.\n\nThey are preserved unchanged as useful exploratory or infrastructure work.\nThey may be cited only by a future task explicitly marked `Infrastructure\nonly`; they cannot establish a manuscript-derived hypothesis or conclusion.\n\n## Interpretation rule\n\nWhen an older document conflicts with `AGENTS.md` or `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`, the\nnew manuscript-only policy governs all future task packets and reports. No\nhistorical label retroactively changes the source basis of a checked Lean\ntheorem.\n\n### PROGRESS.md\n# Formalization progress\n\n## Context policy (2026-08-05)\n\nAll future manuscript-facing formalization takes its context only from\n`SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`, with exact locators in the manuscript `main.tex`. Earlier\ngLU/refinement/layer-coherence artifacts remain available only as explicitly\nlabelled legacy infrastructure; they are not manuscript hypotheses. See\n`AGENTS.md` and `CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`.\n\nThe manuscript-facing module sequence is now `Formalization.Manuscript`:\nsource boundary, finite model, algebraic limit, product state, conditional GNS\nbridge, trace dichotomy, and only then the pending theorem contract. Existing\nimplementation files remain in place and are imported through facades.\n\n- P17 — under a supplied finite adapted-coordinate/word realization, the\n  finite pure/mixed product state is exactly the stabilizer indicator on every\n  supplied source Pauli basis word.\n- P18 — a supplied finite matrix-factor tensor decomposition transports the\n  finite product trace to an algebraic tracial state with its elementary-tensor\n  formula.\n- P19 — a supplied C-star model plus compatible states packages the existing\n  GNS unitary and algebraic commutant transport into one bridge interface.\n- P23 — supplied coherent finite-stage star equivalences induce the infinite\n  algebraic direct-limit regional equivalence `alpha`, with finite-stage and\n  inverse formulas. No C-star or von Neumann extension is claimed.\n- P24 — compatible target-stage algebraic states pull back along `alpha` to a\n  unique algebraic direct-limit state with its exact finite-stage expectation\n  formula and conditional stabilizer-indicator corollary.\n- P25 — supplied C-star completions and an extension of `alpha` yield an\n  isometric continuous C-star equivalence and continuous state pullback;\n  existence of those completions remains an explicit assumption.\n\n## Checked Lean results\n\n- `TPT-001` / `TPT-002` — an algebraic tracial state pulls back along a\n  complex `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- TPT-003 — algebraic traciality is reflected as well as preserved by a\n  complex star-algebra equivalence; hence a nontracial product state cannot\n  become tracial after an algebraic coordinate identification.\n- `FMT-001` — the normalized trace on a nonempty finite full matrix algebra is\n  an algebraic tracial state.  Positivity is proved by the explicit\n  trace-of-a-star-square sum-of-squares identity.\n- `FSC-001` — a finite corner equipped with a star isomorphism to a full matrix\n  algebra inherits that algebraic tracial state.\n- `FTT-001` — the finite algebraic tensor product of two matrix factors is\n  identified by the Kronecker star isomorphism, and its normalized trace\n  factorizes on elementary tensors; tensoring with a matrix identity preserves\n  the trace.\n- `FAM-001` — if `R` has an algebraic tracial state, then its finite matrix\n  amplification `Matrix n n R` has the normalized diagonal tracial state.\n  Positivity is checked as a finite sum of coefficient-algebra star-squares.\n  An explicit `StarAlgEquiv` transports this finite type-I product trace to a\n  target algebra.\n- `TMT-001` — the finite type-I product is now identified canonically, not\n  supplied as an assumption: `Matrix n n ℂ ⊗[ℂ] R` is star-isomorphic to\n  `Matrix n n R`.  Its trace factors on elementary tensors as the normalized\n  matrix trace times the coefficient trace.\n- `DTT-001` — the actual dyadic finite-stage system is constructed as\n  `A₀ = ℂ`, `Aₙ₊₁ = Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) Aₙ`, with diagonal identity-qubit\n  embeddings.  Its normalized traces are compatible with every stage map,\n  and their algebraic directed limit carries the induced algebraic tracial\n  state.\n- FPU-001 — for every nonempty finite matrix factor, its algebraic tensor\n  product with the concrete dyadic algebraic limit carries the checked product\n  tracial state.  The theorem includes the elementary-tensor formula\n  tr_p(a) times qubitUHFTrace(u).\n- FPU-002 — any supplied regional `StarAlgEquiv` into that finite-pure\n  product model induces an algebraic tracial state on the regional algebra,\n  with the checked formula on inverse images of elementary tensors.\n- `AIT-001` — compatible unital cyclic finite-stage functionals glue to a\n  unital cyclic linear functional on mathlib's algebraic directed direct\n  limit.\n- `AIT-002` — compatible stagewise star-algebra maps induce an involutive,\n  additive, multiplication-reversing star on that algebraic direct limit.\n  Compatible algebraic tracial states then glue to an ordinary algebraic\n  tracial state there.\n- `SPC-001` — an adapted Hamel basis identifies the label space with finitely\n  supported X/Z coordinate labels and maps the singleton coordinate labels to\n  the chosen basis vectors.\n- ASC-001 — for a selected set of Z coordinates, Lean now identifies the\n  corresponding coordinate subspace both as the span of those Z basis\n  vectors and as the finite words with no X coordinate and no Z coordinate\n  outside the selected set.  This is the exact linear selection criterion\n  required before comparing the regional stabilizer state with the\n  pure/mixed product state.\n- FQS-001 — Lean now reads the recursive finite-qubit Pauli label as its\n  finite X/Z coordinate list and proves that the pure/mixed product-state\n  indicator is exactly the selected-Z condition: a pure coordinate has no X\n  exponent, and a mixed coordinate is the identity label.  Under an arbitrary\n  supplied adapted coordinate basis, that condition is literal membership in\n  the selected-Z stabilizer submodule.  The coherent direct-limit state theorem\n  has a corresponding selected-Z indicator form on source basis words.\n- `PCC-001` — a phase-corrected additive map of Pauli labels respects word\n  multiplication and star exactly when it satisfies explicit multiplier and\n  involution equations.  This isolates the cocycle data that symplecticity\n  alone does not supply.  The cross-algebra word-basis extension is now also\n  checked: its two compatibility equations and a specified Pauli-word basis\n  yield a genuine `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- `SCC-001` — the quotient of two multiplier phases is an additive character,\n  calibrating by an additive character preserves the multiplier equation, and\n  every additive character on a vector subspace extends to the ambient vector\n  space along a complementary subspace.  This is the exact algebraic\n  stabilizer-sign calibration: the finite-field character is self-conjugate,\n  and calibration preserves both Pauli-word compatibility equations.  The\n  concrete finite-support quadratic phase is now independently formalized;\n  relating it to the actual regional stabilizer convention remains separate.\n- QPR-001 — the explicit ZMod 4 finite-support quadratic refinement has its\n  polarization and diagonal identities fully checked.  Its discrepancy pairing\n  is additive in both words, symmetric for a symmetric discrepancy, and has\n  additive order two.  These facts instantiate the cross-algebra Pauli-word\n  multiplication and involution certificates and, with a supplied word-basis\n  linear equivalence, a `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- QPT-001 — the concrete finite-support phase theorem now composes that\n  `StarAlgEquiv` with the finite-pure times qubit-UHF trace transport: whenever\n  the regional Pauli-word exponents, involution phases, and word-basis map are\n  supplied, Lean constructs both the star-algebra equivalence and its induced\n  algebraic tracial state with the elementary-tensor product formula.  The\n  Pauli-word interface has been weakened from `Ring` to `Semiring`, the exact\n  structure carried by the concrete algebraic UHF direct limit.\n- PTO-001 — two isometries whose range projections sum to the identity rule\n  out even an algebraic unital cyclic trace.  This is the checked obstruction\n  behind the infinite-pure-sector case; the tracial-state theorem is therefore\n  deliberately limited to a finite pure matrix factor.\n- ALT-001 — compatible finite-stage `StarAlgEquiv`s and compatible target\n  algebraic tracial states induce a source-side algebraic tracial state on the\n  corresponding quasi-local direct limit.  The theorem evaluates it exactly\n  on every finite-stage representative.  The concrete finite-pure UHF trace\n  now has the matching finite-stage elementary-tensor formula.\n- FQP-001 — every finite stage of the dyadic qubit tower now has an explicit\n  ordered Pauli-word `StarSystem` and a complex Pauli-word basis.  The\n  identity-qubit embeddings preserve its words, basis vectors, multiplication\n  multipliers, and star multipliers under coherent label maps.  Thus the UHF\n  target-side finite-stage word hypotheses are no longer abstract assumptions;\n  assembling them into one global target word system remains open.\n- PMS-001 — for every Boolean sequence specifying whether each newly adjoined\n  qubit is pure or maximally mixed, the corresponding finite product\n  functionals are algebraic states, are compatible with all dyadic embeddings,\n  and induce an algebraic state on the UHF direct limit.  Their values on every\n  finite Pauli word are proved recursively: pure coordinates permit only zero\n  X exponent, while mixed coordinates permit only the identity word.  The\n  all-mixed specialization is proved equal to the previously checked UHF\n  tracial state.  Lean also proves that even the one-qubit pure specialization\n  is not cyclic and hence not an algebraic tracial state.\n- PTR-001 — this nontraciality is now proved at arbitrary finite and\n  direct-limit coordinates: if any Boolean coordinate is pure, explicit\n  matrix units witness a failure of cyclicity.  Consequently the algebraic\n  UHF product state is tracial if and only if every coordinate is maximally\n  mixed, in which case it is the checked UHF trace.\n- CBL-001 — finite-stage star-algebra equivalences need not be assumed\n  coherent on every algebra element: if the source connecting maps, target\n  vectors, and coordinate maps agree on a specified source basis, Lean proves\n  the full commuting square by linear-basis extensionality.\n- CPS-001 — a phase-corrected finite coordinate system now has a concrete\n  direct-limit state interface.  Coherent source basis transport, phases, and\n  finite Pauli labels imply full-stage equivalence coherence and hence a\n  star-algebra equivalence to the algebraic qubit-UHF direct limit; the induced\n  source state evaluates a basis word as its phase times the pure/mixed Pauli\n  selection indicator.  The all-mixed specialization pulls the UHF trace back\n  to a source algebraic tracial state; more generally its pure/mixed pullback\n  is tracial exactly when every coordinate is mixed.  Constructing the\n  regional inputs remains open.\n- ACS-001 — if supplied finite-stage regional data identify selected target\n  Z-coordinates with the actual source stabilizer words, Lean derives the\n  exact phase-calibrated stabilizer expectation formula on every source basis word.\n  The stronger form states that hypothesis as literal membership in the\n  selected-Z submodule of an adapted coordinate basis.  Thus the remaining\n  geometric work is isolated to constructing and coherently transporting that\n  coordinate data from a separately supplied finite-coordinate realization,\n  rather than to a hidden infinite-algebra assumption. The manuscript does not\n  presently supply that realization; see `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`.\n- ALS-001 — compatible finite-stage star-algebra equivalences transport\n  compatible algebraic states (not only tracial states) to a quasi-local\n  algebraic direct limit.  This is the exact state-level interface required by\n  a coherent regional stabilizer-coordinate construction.\n- WBE-001 — if Pauli words form a complex linear basis, checking a\n  complex-linear map on every pair of basis words proves multiplicativity on\n  all finite sums.  The corresponding conjugate-linear basis argument proves\n  star preservation; a bijective unital map satisfying both checks is packaged\n  as a star-algebra equivalence.\n- ALC-001 — a compatible family of finite-stage star-algebra maps induces a\n  star-algebra map from the algebraic directed limit.  Compatible finite-stage\n  star-algebra equivalences induce a star-algebra equivalence of the two\n  algebraic limits.  The C-star norm extension remains open.\n- CSE-001 — once a source and target are supplied as complex C-star algebras,\n  Lean proves that every complex `StarAlgEquiv` is isometric and therefore\n  continuous with continuous inverse.  A continuous target functional pulls\n  back continuously along it.  This does not construct C-star norms or\n  completions for the algebraic direct-limit models.\n- CCF-001 — the pinned Mathlib supports the finite dyadic stages as genuine\n  C-star algebras and supports closure/minimality once an ambient C-star\n  algebra is supplied.  It does not provide the C-star inductive-limit\n  construction for the algebraic dyadic limit; this is recorded as an\n  explicit missing API rather than hidden behind an axiom.\n- NMC-001 — supplied finite C-star realizations of the coherent coordinate\n  maps are isometric and continuous, and the commuting inclusion squares\n  preserve the norm estimates.  The resulting dense-subalgebra estimate is\n  exactly the analytic input needed before extending the algebraic map to a\n  completion; no completion is constructed here.\n- BMC-001 — for a star subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\n  Lean now constructs the algebraic bicommutant as a concrete\n  `VonNeumannAlgebra`, with monotonicity, minimality, membership, and\n  idempotence lemmas.  The analytic strong/weak double-commutant theorem is\n  still explicitly absent from the pinned Mathlib and remains separate.\n- CCC-001 — from an explicitly supplied isometric star representation of a\n  pre-C-star algebra into bounded operators, Lean constructs the closed\n  operator-range C-star algebra, its isometric dense embedding, and the\n  uniqueness equivalence between two such concrete completions.  The faithful\n  operator representation of the project-specific algebraic limit is still a\n  separate obligation.\n\n## The exact theorem still to prove\n\nThe Overleaf short-range theorem asserts a regional von Neumann algebra\nisomorphism to a type-I factor tensor a hyperfinite type-II1 factor.  It is\nnot yet formalized as a theorem because the supplied manuscript proof leaves\nthe following essential premises open:\n\n1. a finite-support adapted symplectic coordinate map whose finite-stage\n   source bases, phase correction, and