{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,300p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && printf '\\\\n--- live source, main proof ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean | sed -n '60,490p' && printf '\\\\n--- P49A support interfaces ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann.lean | sed -n '60,340p' && printf '\\\\n--- core definitions ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/BicommutantVonNeumannCore.lean | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,300p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && printf '\\\\n--- live source, main proof ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean | sed -n '60,490p' && printf '\\\\n--- P49A support interfaces ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann.lean | sed -n '60,340p' && printf '\\\\n--- core definitions ---\\\\n' && nl -ba AristotleCandidates/BicommutantVonNeumannCore.lean | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review Protocol\n\nUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\nto summarize accomplishments.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n   the remaining risk.\n\n## Universal Checks\n\n- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n  intentionally?\n- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n\n## Code Checks\n\n- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n  fail?\n- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n  stale cache behavior?\n- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n\n## Math Checks\n\n- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n  conditions stated before use?\n- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n  ways?\n- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n  numerical evidence?\n\n## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n\n- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n\n## Computation Checks\n\n- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n  affect the result?\n- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n\n## Closeout Template\n\nUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n\n```text\nFindings:\n- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n\nChecks run:\n- command -> result.\n\nResidual risk:\n- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n\nCleanup:\n- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n```\n\n--- live source, main proof ---\n    60\t`VonNeumannBicommutant.mem_strongClosure_iff` makes the finite-neighbourhood form explicit.\n    61\t-/\n    62\t\n    63\tuniverse u\n    64\t\n    65\topen Filter Topology ContinuousLinearMap\n    66\t\n    67\tnamespace VonNeumannBicommutant\n    68\t\n    69\t/-! ## The strong operator closure of a set of operators -/\n    70\t\n    71\tsection StrongClosure\n    72\t\n    73\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H]\n    74\t\n    75\t/-- The **strong operator closure** of a set `K` of bounded operators: the closure of `K` inside\n    76\t`StrongOperator.SOT H`, Mathlib's strong-operator-topologized copy of `H →L[ℂ] H`. -/\n    77\tdef strongClosure (K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H) :=\n    78\t  {T | StrongOperator.toSOT T ∈\n    79\t    closure {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ K}}\n    80\t\n    81\ttheorem subset_strongClosure (K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : K ⊆ strongClosure K := fun _ hT =>\n    82\t  @subset_closure (StrongOperator.SOT H) _ _ _ hT\n    83\t\n    84\t/-- The strong closure is contained in any strongly closed set containing `K`.  (`P49A`'s\n    85\t`StrongOperator.IsStronglyClosed` is used as the notion of strong closedness.) -/\n    86\ttheorem strongClosure_subset {K C : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} (hKC : K ⊆ C)\n    87\t    (hC : StrongOperator.IsStronglyClosed C) : strongClosure K ⊆ C := by\n    88\t  intro T hT\n    89\t  have h : closure {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ K}\n    90\t      ⊆ {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ C} :=\n    91\t    closure_minimal (fun B hB => hKC hB) hC\n    92\t  exact h hT\n    93\t\n    94\t/-- **Finite-neighbourhood form, sufficiency.**  If `T` can be approximated by elements of `K`\n    95\tsimultaneously on every finite set of vectors, then `T` lies in the strong closure of `K`. -/\n    96\ttheorem mem_strongClosure_of_approx {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n    97\t    (h : ∀ (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε → ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε) :\n    98\t    T ∈ strongClosure K := by\n    99\t  classical\n   100\t  rw [strongClosure, Set.mem_setOf_eq, mem_closure_iff_nhds]\n   101\t  intro U hU\n   102\t  have hind := PointwiseConvergenceCLM.isEmbedding_coeFn (σ := RingHom.id ℂ) (E := H) (F := H)\n   103\t  rw [hind.isInducing.nhds_eq_comap, Filter.mem_comap] at hU\n   104\t  obtain ⟨V, hV, hVU⟩ := hU\n   105\t  rw [nhds_pi, Filter.mem_pi] at hV\n   106\t  obtain ⟨I, hI, t, ht, htV⟩ := hV\n   107\t  have hr : ∀ x : H, ∃ r : ℝ, 0 < r ∧ Metric.ball (T x) r ⊆ t x := fun x =>\n   108\t    Metric.mem_nhds_iff.1 (ht x)\n   109\t  choose r hr0 hrt using hr\n   110\t  set s : Finset H := hI.toFinset with hs\n   111\t  set ε : ℝ := (insert (1 : ℝ) (s.image r)).min' (by simp) with hε\n   112\t  have hε0 : 0 < ε := by\n   113\t    have hmem := Finset.min'_mem (insert (1 : ℝ) (s.image r)) (by simp)\n   114\t    rcases Finset.mem_insert.1 hmem with h1 | h1\n   115\t    · rw [hε, h1]; norm_num\n   116\t    · obtain ⟨x, _, hx⟩ := Finset.mem_image.1 h1\n   117\t      rw [hε, ← hx]; exact hr0 x\n   118\t  have hεle : ∀ x ∈ s, ε ≤ r x := fun x hx =>\n   119\t    Finset.min'_le _ _ (Finset.mem_insert_of_mem (Finset.mem_image_of_mem r hx))\n   120\t  obtain ⟨a, haK, ha⟩ := h s ε hε0\n   121\t  refine ⟨StrongOperator.toSOT a, hVU (htV ?_), haK⟩\n   122\t  intro x hx\n   123\t  have hxs : x ∈ s := by rw [hs]; simpa using hx\n   124\t  refine hrt x ?_\n   125\t  rw [Metric.mem_ball, dist_eq_norm]\n   126\t  calc ‖a x - T x‖ = ‖T x - a x‖ := by rw [norm_sub_rev]\n   127\t    _ < ε := ha x hxs\n   128\t    _ ≤ r x := hεle x hxs\n   129\t\n   130\t/-- **Finite-neighbourhood form, necessity.**  An element of the strong closure of `K` is\n   131\tapproximated by elements of `K` simultaneously on every finite set of vectors. -/\n   132\ttheorem approx_of_mem_strongClosure {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n   133\t    (hT : T ∈ strongClosure K) (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) :\n   134\t    ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε := by\n   135\t  classical\n   136\t  rw [strongClosure, Set.mem_setOf_eq, mem_closure_iff_nhds] at hT\n   137\t  have hind := PointwiseConvergenceCLM.isEmbedding_coeFn (σ := RingHom.id ℂ) (E := H) (F := H)\n   138\t  set V : Set (H → H) := Set.pi (F : Set H) (fun x => Metric.ball (T x) ε) with hV\n   139\t  have hVnhds : V ∈ 𝓝 (fun x => T x : H → H) := by\n   140\t    rw [nhds_pi]\n   141\t    exact Filter.pi_mem_pi F.finite_toSet (fun x _ => Metric.ball_mem_nhds _ hε)\n   142\t  set U : Set (StrongOperator.SOT H) := (fun B : StrongOperator.SOT H => (B : H → H)) ⁻¹' V\n   143\t    with hU\n   144\t  have hUnhds : U ∈ 𝓝 (StrongOperator.toSOT T) := by\n   145\t    rw [hind.isInducing.nhds_eq_comap]\n   146\t    exact Filter.mem_comap.2 ⟨V, hVnhds, subset_rfl⟩\n   147\t  obtain ⟨B, hBU, hBK⟩ := hT U hUnhds\n   148\t  refine ⟨StrongOperator.ofSOT B, hBK, fun x hx => ?_⟩\n   149\t  have h2 := hBU x hx\n   150\t  rw [Metric.mem_ball, dist_eq_norm] at h2\n   151\t  rw [norm_sub_rev]\n   152\t  exact h2\n   153\t\n   154\t/-- **Membership in the strong closure is exactly simultaneous finite approximation.**  This is\n   155\tthe \"arbitrary finite strong neighbourhood\" form of the closure. -/\n   156\ttheorem mem_strongClosure_iff {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n   157\t    T ∈ strongClosure K ↔\n   158\t      ∀ (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε → ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε :=\n   159\t  ⟨fun hT F ε hε => approx_of_mem_strongClosure hT F ε hε, mem_strongClosure_of_approx⟩\n   160\t\n   161\t/-- Net form: the strong closure contains every strong limit of a net taken in `K`. -/\n   162\ttheorem mem_strongClosure_of_stronglyTendsto {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {ι : Type*} {l : Filter ι}\n   163\t    [l.NeBot] {S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H} (hmem : ∀ᶠ i in l, S i ∈ K)\n   164\t    (hS : StrongOperator.StronglyTendsto l S T) : T ∈ strongClosure K := by\n   165\t  refine mem_strongClosure_of_approx fun F ε hε => ?_\n   166\t  have hall : ∀ᶠ i in l, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - S i x‖ < ε := by\n   167\t    refine (Filter.eventually_all_finset F).2 fun x _ => ?_\n   168\t    have h0 : Tendsto (fun i => ‖T x - S i x‖) l (𝓝 0) := by\n   169\t      have := (hS x).const_sub (T x)\n   170\t      simpa using this.norm\n   171\t    exact h0.eventually (eventually_lt_nhds hε)\n   172\t  obtain ⟨i, hiK, hi⟩ := (hmem.and hall).exists\n   173\t  exact ⟨S i, hiK, hi⟩\n   174\t\n   175\tend StrongClosure\n   176\t\n   177\t/-! ## Invariant subspaces and orthogonal projections -/\n   178\t\n   179\tsection Invariant\n   180\t\n   181\tvariable {E : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℂ E] [CompleteSpace E]\n   182\t\n   183\t/-- If a closed subspace `K` is invariant under `b` and under `star b`, then the orthogonal\n   184\tprojection onto `K` commutes with `b`. -/\n   185\ttheorem commute_starProjection_of_invariant (K : Submodule ℂ E) [K.HasOrthogonalProjection]\n   186\t    (b : E →L[ℂ] E) (h1 : ∀ x ∈ K, b x ∈ K) (h2 : ∀ x ∈ K, (star b) x ∈ K) :\n   187\t    b * K.starProjection = K.starProjection * b := by\n   188\t  have horth : ∀ y ∈ Kᗮ, b y ∈ Kᗮ := by\n   189\t    intro y hy\n   190\t    rw [Submodule.mem_orthogonal]\n   191\t    intro z hz\n   192\t    have h : inner ℂ z (b y) = inner ℂ ((star b) z) y := by\n   193\t      rw [ContinuousLinearMap.star_eq_adjoint, ContinuousLinearMap.adjoint_inner_left]\n   194\t    rw [h]\n   195\t    exact (Submodule.mem_orthogonal K y).1 hy _ (h2 z hz)\n   196\t  ext x\n   197\t  simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply]\n   198\t  have h3 : b (K.starProjection x) ∈ K := h1 _ (Submodule.starProjection_apply_mem K x)\n   199\t  have h4 : b (x - K.starProjection x) ∈ Kᗮ :=\n   200\t    horth _ (Submodule.sub_starProjection_mem_orthogonal x)\n   201\t  have key : K.starProjection (b x)\n   202\t      = K.starProjection (b (K.starProjection x))\n   203\t        + K.starProjection (b (x - K.starProjection x)) := by\n   204\t    rw [← map_add, ← map_add]\n   205\t    congr 1\n   206\t    abel_nf\n   207\t  rw [key, Submodule.starProjection_eq_self_iff.2 h3,\n   208\t    (Submodule.starProjection_apply_eq_zero_iff K).mpr h4, add_zero]\n   209\t\n   210\tend Invariant\n   211\t\n   212\t/-! ## Finite amplifications -/\n   213\t\n   214\tsection Amplification\n   215\t\n   216\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H]\n   217\t\n   218\t/-- The `n`-fold Hilbert-space amplification `H ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ H` of `H`. -/\n   219\tabbrev Amp (H : Type u) [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] (n : ℕ) :=\n   220\t  PiLp 2 (fun _ : Fin n => H)\n   221\t\n   222\t/-- The diagonal amplification of a bounded operator. -/\n   223\tnoncomputable def ampOp (n : ℕ) (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n :=\n   224\t  (PiLp.continuousLinearEquiv 2 ℂ (fun _ : Fin n => H)).symm.toContinuousLinearMap ∘L\n   225\t    (ContinuousLinearMap.pi (fun i => T ∘L PiLp.proj 2 (fun _ : Fin n => H) i))\n   226\t\n   227\t@[simp] theorem ampOp_apply (n : ℕ) (T : H →L[ℂ] H) (x : Amp H n) (i : Fin n) :\n   228\t    (ampOp n T x) i = T (x i) := rfl\n   229\t\n   230\t/-- The `j`-th coordinate embedding `H → Amp H n`. -/\n   231\tnoncomputable def ampIn (n : ℕ) (j : Fin n) : H →L[ℂ] Amp H n :=\n   232\t  (PiLp.continuousLinearEquiv 2 ℂ (fun _ : Fin n => H)).symm.toContinuousLinearMap ∘L\n   233\t    (ContinuousLinearMap.single ℂ (fun _ : Fin n => H) j)\n   234\t\n   235\t@[simp] theorem ampIn_apply (n : ℕ) (j : Fin n) (y : H) (i : Fin n) :\n   236\t    (ampIn n j y) i = (Pi.single j y : ∀ _ : Fin n, H) i := rfl\n   237\t\n   238\ttheorem amp_sum_apply {n : ℕ} (s : Finset (Fin n)) (f : Fin n → Amp H n) (i : Fin n) :\n   239\t    (∑ j ∈ s, f j) i = ∑ j ∈ s, (f j) i := by\n   240\t  simp\n   241\t\n   242\ttheorem amp_sub_apply {n : ℕ} (x y : Amp H n) (i : Fin n) : (x - y) i = x i - y i := rfl\n   243\t\n   244\t/-- Every element of `Amp H n` is the sum of its coordinate embeddings. -/\n   245\ttheorem sum_ampIn (n : ℕ) (x : Amp H n) : ∑ j, ampIn n j (x j) = x := by\n   246\t  classical\n   247\t  ext i\n   248\t  rw [amp_sum_apply]\n   249\t  simp\n   250\t\n   251\ttheorem ampOp_one (n : ℕ) : ampOp n (1 : H →L[ℂ] H) = 1 := by ext x i; simp\n   252\t\n   253\ttheorem ampOp_mul (n : ℕ) (S T : H →L[ℂ] H) : ampOp n (S * T) = ampOp n S * ampOp n T := by\n   254\t  ext x i; simp\n   255\t\n   256\ttheorem ampOp_add (n : ℕ) (S T : H →L[ℂ] H) : ampOp n (S + T) = ampOp n S + ampOp n T := by\n   257\t  ext x i\n   258\t  simp\n   259\t\n   260\ttheorem ampOp_smul (n : ℕ) (c : ℂ) (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : ampOp n (c • T) = c • ampOp n T := by\n   261\t  ext x i; simp\n   262\t\n   263\tsection Complete\n   264\t\n   265\tvariable [CompleteSpace H]\n   266\t\n   267\ttheorem ampOp_star (n : ℕ) (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : ampOp n (star T) = star (ampOp n T) := by\n   268\t  rw [ContinuousLinearMap.star_eq_adjoint (ampOp n T), ContinuousLinearMap.eq_adjoint_iff]\n   269\t  intro x y\n   270\t  simp only [PiLp.inner_apply, ampOp_apply]\n   271\t  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_\n   272\t  rw [ContinuousLinearMap.star_eq_adjoint, ContinuousLinearMap.adjoint_inner_left]\n   273\t\n   274\t/-- The diagonal amplification, packaged as a unital star algebra homomorphism. -/\n   275\tnoncomputable def ampHom (n : ℕ) : (H →L[ℂ] H) →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n) where\n   276\t  toFun := ampOp n\n   277\t  map_one' := ampOp_one n\n   278\t  map_mul' := ampOp_mul n\n   279\t  map_zero' := by ext x i; simp\n   280\t  map_add' := ampOp_add n\n   281\t  commutes' r := by\n   282\t    have h : ampOp n ((algebraMap ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) r) = ampOp n (r • (1 : H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   283\t      rw [Algebra.algebraMap_eq_smul_one]\n   284\t    rw [h, ampOp_smul, ampOp_one, Algebra.algebraMap_eq_smul_one]\n   285\t  map_star' := ampOp_star n\n   286\t\n   287\t@[simp] theorem ampHom_apply (n : ℕ) (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : (ampHom (H := H) n) T = ampOp n T := rfl\n   288\t\n   289\tend Complete\n   290\t\n   291\tend Amplification\n   292\t\n   293\t/-! ## The matrix-entry lemma -/\n   294\t\n   295\tsection Entries\n   296\t\n   297\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H]\n   298\t\n   299\t/-- The `(i,j)` matrix entry of an operator on the amplification `Amp H n`. -/\n   300\tnoncomputable def ampEntry (n : ℕ) (S : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n) (i j : Fin n) : H →L[ℂ] H :=\n   301\t  (PiLp.proj 2 (fun _ : Fin n => H) i) ∘L S ∘L (ampIn n j)\n   302\t\n   303\t@[simp] theorem ampEntry_apply (n : ℕ) (S : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n) (i j : Fin n) (y : H) :\n   304\t    ampEntry n S i j y = (S (ampIn n j y)) i := rfl\n   305\t\n   306\ttheorem ampIn_comp (n : ℕ) (j : Fin n) (a : H →L[ℂ] H) (y : H) :\n   307\t    ampIn n j (a y) = ampOp n a (ampIn n j y) := by\n   308\t  classical\n   309\t  ext i\n   310\t  simp only [ampIn_apply, ampOp_apply, Pi.single_apply]\n   311\t  split_ifs with h\n   312\t  · rfl\n   313\t  · simp\n   314\t\n   315\t/-- If `S` commutes with the amplification of every element of `A`, then each matrix entry of `S`\n   316\tlies in the commutant of `A`. -/\n   317\ttheorem ampEntry_mem_centralizer {A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {n : ℕ} {S : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n}\n   318\t    (hS : ∀ a ∈ A, S * ampOp n a = ampOp n a * S) (i j : Fin n) :\n   319\t    ampEntry n S i j ∈ Set.centralizer A := by\n   320\t  intro a ha\n   321\t  ext y\n   322\t  have hcomm := congrArg (fun t : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n => t (ampIn n j y)) (hS a ha)\n   323\t  simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply] at hcomm\n   324\t  have h1 : ampEntry n S i j (a y) = (S (ampOp n a (ampIn n j y))) i := by\n   325\t    rw [ampEntry_apply, ampIn_comp]\n   326\t  simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply, ampEntry_apply, h1, hcomm, ampOp_apply]\n   327\t\n   328\t/-- **Key amplification step.**  If `T` lies in the bicommutant of `A`, then its amplification\n   329\tcommutes with every operator commuting with the amplification of `A`. -/\n   330\ttheorem ampOp_commute_of_mem_bicommutant {A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n   331\t    (hT : T ∈ Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer A)) {n : ℕ} {S : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n}\n   332\t    (hS : ∀ a ∈ A, S * ampOp n a = ampOp n a * S) :\n   333\t    S * ampOp n T = ampOp n T * S := by\n   334\t  classical\n   335\t  have hent : ∀ i j : Fin n, ampEntry n S i j * T = T * ampEntry n S i j := fun i j =>\n   336\t    hT _ (ampEntry_mem_centralizer hS i j)\n   337\t  ext x i\n   338\t  simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply, ampOp_apply]\n   339\t  -- expand `x` and `ampOp n T x` in coordinates\n   340\t  have hx : x = ∑ j, ampIn n j (x j) := (sum_ampIn n x).symm\n   341\t  have hTx : ampOp n T x = ∑ j, ampIn n j (T (x j)) := by\n   342\t    have := sum_ampIn n (ampOp n T x)\n   343\t    simpa using this.symm\n   344\t  have hSx : (S x) i = ∑ j, ampEntry n S i j (x j) := by\n   345\t    conv_lhs => rw [hx]\n   346\t    rw [map_sum, amp_sum_apply]\n   347\t    rfl\n   348\t  have hSTx : (S (ampOp n T x)) i = ∑ j, ampEntry n S i j (T (x j)) := by\n   349\t    conv_lhs => rw [hTx]\n   350\t    rw [map_sum, amp_sum_apply]\n   351\t    rfl\n   352\t  rw [hSTx, hSx, map_sum]\n   353\t  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun j _ => ?_\n   354\t  have h := congrArg (fun t : H →L[ℂ] H => t (x j)) (hent i j)\n   355\t  simpa using h\n   356\t\n   357\tend Entries\n   358\t\n   359\t/-! ## The bicommutant theorem -/\n   360\t\n   361\tsection Main\n   362\t\n   363\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H]\n   364\t\n   365\t/-- **Simultaneous approximation on a finite family of vectors.**  Every element of the\n   366\tbicommutant of a star subalgebra `A` of `B(H)` is approximated, on any finite family of vectors\n   367\tand to any accuracy, by an element of `A`. -/\n   368\ttheorem exists_mem_approx_on_fin (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n   369\t    (hT : T ∈ Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)))) (n : ℕ) (v : Fin n → H)\n   370\t    {ε : ℝ} (hε : 0 < ε) :\n   371\t    ∃ a ∈ A, ∀ i, ‖T (v i) - a (v i)‖ < ε := by\n   372\t  classical\n   373\t  set ξ : Amp H n := WithLp.toLp 2 v\n   374\t  have hξ_apply : ∀ i, ξ i = v i := fun _ => rfl\n   375\t  -- the cyclic submodule `A ξ`\n   376\t  set φ : (H →L[ℂ] H) →ₗ[ℂ] Amp H n :=\n   377\t    (ContinuousLinearMap.apply ℂ (Amp H n) ξ).toLinearMap ∘ₗ (ampHom n).toLinearMap\n   378\t  set M : Submodule ℂ (Amp H n) := Submodule.map φ A.toSubmodule\n   379\t  have hM_mem : ∀ y, y ∈ M ↔ ∃ b ∈ A, ampOp n b ξ = y := by\n   380\t    intro y\n   381\t    constructor\n   382\t    · rintro ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩; exact ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩\n   383\t    · rintro ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩; exact ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩\n   384\t  set K : Submodule ℂ (Amp H n) := M.topologicalClosure\n   385\t  have hKclosed : IsClosed (K : Set (Amp H n)) := M.isClosed_topologicalClosure\n   386\t  have hKcoe : (K : Set (Amp H n)) = closure (M : Set (Amp H n)) := M.topologicalClosure_coe\n   387\t  haveI : CompleteSpace K := hKclosed.completeSpace_coe\n   388\t  haveI : K.HasOrthogonalProjection := inferInstance\n   389\t  -- `ξ` belongs to the cyclic subspace, by unitality of `A`\n   390\t  have hξK : ξ ∈ K := by\n   391\t    refine Submodule.le_topologicalClosure M ?_\n   392\t    refine (hM_mem ξ).2 ⟨1, A.one_mem, ?_⟩\n   393\t    rw [ampOp_one]\n   394\t    rfl\n   395\t  -- the cyclic subspace is invariant under `A`\n   396\t  have hinv : ∀ a ∈ A, ∀ y ∈ K, ampOp n a y ∈ K := by\n   397\t    intro a ha y hy\n   398\t    have hMmaps : Set.MapsTo (ampOp n a) (M : Set (Amp H n)) (M : Set (Amp H n)) := by\n   399\t      intro z hz\n   400\t      obtain ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩ := (hM_mem z).1 hz\n   401\t      refine (hM_mem _).2 ⟨a * b, mul_mem ha hb, ?_⟩\n   402\t      rw [ampOp_mul]\n   403\t      rfl\n   404\t    have hmaps := hMmaps.closure (ampOp n a).continuous\n   405\t    rw [← hKcoe] at hmaps\n   406\t    exact hmaps (show y ∈ (K : Set (Amp H n)) from hy)\n   407\t  -- hence the orthogonal projection onto it commutes with the amplification of `A`\n   408\t  have hPcomm : ∀ a ∈ A, K.starProjection * ampOp n a = ampOp n a * K.starProjection := by\n   409\t    intro a ha\n   410\t    refine (commute_starProjection_of_invariant K (ampOp n a) (hinv a ha) ?_).symm\n   411\t    intro y hy\n   412\t    rw [← ampOp_star]\n   413\t    exact hinv (star a) (star_mem ha) y hy\n   414\t  -- and therefore with the amplification of `T`\n   415\t  have hTcomm : K.starProjection * ampOp n T = ampOp n T * K.starProjection :=\n   416\t    ampOp_commute_of_mem_bicommutant hT hPcomm\n   417\t  -- so `ampOp n T ξ` lies in the cyclic subspace\n   418\t  have hTξ : ampOp n T ξ ∈ K := by\n   419\t    have h1 : K.starProjection ξ = ξ := Submodule.starProjection_eq_self_iff.2 hξK\n   420\t    have h2 := congrArg (fun t : Amp H n →L[ℂ] Amp H n => t ξ) hTcomm\n   421\t    simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply, h1] at h2\n   422\t    rw [← h2]\n   423\t    exact Submodule.starProjection_apply_mem K _\n   424\t  -- approximate it by an element of `A ξ`\n   425\t  have hTξ' : ampOp n T ξ ∈ closure (M : Set (Amp H n)) := by rw [← hKcoe]; exact hTξ\n   426\t  rw [Metric.mem_closure_iff] at hTξ'\n   427\t  obtain ⟨y, hyM, hy⟩ := hTξ' ε hε\n   428\t  obtain ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩ := (hM_mem y).1 hyM\n   429\t  refine ⟨a, ha, fun i => ?_⟩\n   430\t  have hnorm : ‖ampOp n T ξ - ampOp n a ξ‖ < ε := by\n   431\t    rwa [← dist_eq_norm]\n   432\t  calc ‖T (v i) - a (v i)‖\n   433\t      = ‖(ampOp n T ξ - ampOp n a ξ) i‖ := by\n   434\t        rw [amp_sub_apply, ampOp_apply, ampOp_apply, hξ_apply]\n   435\t    _ ≤ ‖ampOp n T ξ - ampOp n a ξ‖ := PiLp.norm_apply_le _ i\n   436\t    _ < ε := hnorm\n   437\t\n   438\t/-- The bicommutant is contained in the strong operator closure. -/\n   439\ttheorem bicommutant_subset_strongClosure (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   440\t    Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)))\n   441\t      ⊆ strongClosure (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   442\t  classical\n   443\t  intro T hT\n   444\t  refine mem_strongClosure_of_approx fun F ε hε => ?_\n   445\t  set e := F.equivFin\n   446\t  obtain ⟨a, ha, hap⟩ :=\n   447\t    exists_mem_approx_on_fin A hT F.card (fun i => ((e.symm i : F) : H)) hε\n   448\t  refine ⟨a, ha, fun x hx => ?_⟩\n   449\t  have hxv : x = ((e.symm (e ⟨x, hx⟩) : F) : H) := by simp\n   450\t  have hx' := hap (e ⟨x, hx⟩)\n   451\t  rwa [← hxv] at hx'\n   452\t\n   453\t/-- The strong operator closure is contained in the bicommutant. -/\n   454\ttheorem strongClosure_subset_bicommutant (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   455\t    strongClosure (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))\n   456\t      ⊆ Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) :=\n   457\t  strongClosure_subset Set.subset_centralizer_centralizer\n   458\t    (StrongOperator.isStronglyClosed_centralizer _)\n   459\t\n   460\t/-- **The analytic von Neumann bicommutant (double commutant) theorem.**\n   461\t\n   462\tFor a unital complex star subalgebra `A` of the bounded operators on a complex Hilbert space `H`\n   463\t(unitality is automatic for `StarSubalgebra ℂ _`), the strong operator closure of `A` coincides\n   464\twith its bicommutant `A''`. -/\n   465\ttheorem strongClosure_eq_bicommutant (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   466\t    strongClosure (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))\n   467\t      = Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) :=\n   468\t  Set.Subset.antisymm (strongClosure_subset_bicommutant A) (bicommutant_subset_strongClosure A)\n   469\t\n   470\t/-- The same statement phrased with the concrete von Neumann algebra `A''` produced by the\n   471\talgebraic bicommutant core of `P49A`'s companion file. -/\n   472\ttheorem strongClosure_eq_vonNeumannGenerated (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   473\t    strongClosure (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))\n   474\t      = (StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   475\t  rw [strongClosure_eq_bicommutant, StarSubalgebra.coe_vonNeumannGenerated]\n   476\t\n   477\t/-- A concrete von Neumann algebra is exactly the strong operator closure of itself; equivalently,\n   478\ta strongly closed unital star subalgebra equals its own bicommutant. -/\n   479\ttheorem strongClosure_eq_self_iff (A : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   480\t    strongClosure (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) = (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) ↔\n   481\t      ∃ M : VonNeumannAlgebra H, (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) = (A : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   482\t  constructor\n   483\t  · intro h\n   484\t    refine ⟨StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated A, ?_⟩\n   485\t    rw [← strongClosure_eq_vonNeumannGenerated, h]\n   486\t  · rintro ⟨M, hM⟩\n   487\t    rw [strongClosure_eq_bicommutant, ← hM, M.centralizer_centralizer]\n   488\t\n   489\tend Main\n   490\t\n\n--- P49A support interfaces ---\n    60\t\n    61\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H]\n    62\t\n    63\t/-! ### The strong operator topology on `H →L[ℂ] H` -/\n    64\t\n    65\t/-- The bounded operators on `H`, carrying the **strong operator topology** (the topology of\n    66\tpointwise convergence).  This is Mathlib's `PointwiseConvergenceCLM`, which is a type synonym of\n    67\t`H →L[ℂ] H`. -/\n    68\tabbrev SOT (H : Type u) [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] :=\n    69\t  H →Lₚₜ[ℂ] H\n    70\t\n    71\t/-- View a bounded operator as an element of the strong-operator-topologized space. -/\n    72\tdef toSOT (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : SOT H := T\n    73\t\n    74\t/-- View an element of the strong-operator-topologized space as a bounded operator. -/\n    75\tdef ofSOT (T : SOT H) : H →L[ℂ] H := T\n    76\t\n    77\t@[simp] theorem ofSOT_toSOT (T : H →L[ℂ] H) : ofSOT (toSOT T) = T := rfl\n    78\t\n    79\t@[simp] theorem toSOT_ofSOT (T : SOT H) : toSOT (ofSOT T) = T := rfl\n    80\t\n    81\t@[simp] theorem toSOT_apply (T : H →L[ℂ] H) (x : H) : (toSOT T) x = T x := rfl\n    82\t\n    83\t@[simp] theorem ofSOT_apply (T : SOT H) (x : H) : (ofSOT T) x = T x := rfl\n    84\t\n    85\ttheorem toSOT_injective : Function.Injective (toSOT (H := H)) := fun _ _ h => h\n    86\t\n    87\t/-- The strong-operator-topologized space is Hausdorff, so strong limits are unique. -/\n    88\tinstance : T2Space (SOT H) := inferInstance\n    89\t\n    90\t/-! ### Strong convergence of a net of operators -/\n    91\t\n    92\t/-- **Strong (operator) convergence** of a filter-indexed family: `T i → A` pointwise in the norm\n    93\tof `H`.  For `l = atTop` this is convergence of a sequence, for a general `l` that of a net. -/\n    94\tdef StronglyTendsto {ι : Type v} (l : Filter ι) (T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)) (A : H →L[ℂ] H) : Prop :=\n    95\t  ∀ x : H, Tendsto (fun i => T i x) l (𝓝 (A x))\n    96\t\n    97\t/-- **(1) Core conclusion.**  A net of bounded operators converges in the strong operator\n    98\ttopology exactly when it converges pointwise in the norm of `H`. -/\n    99\ttheorem stronglyTendsto_iff_tendsto_sot {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   100\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n   101\t    StronglyTendsto l T A ↔ Tendsto (fun i => toSOT (T i)) l (𝓝 (toSOT A)) :=\n   102\t  PointwiseConvergenceCLM.tendsto_iff_forall_tendsto.symm\n   103\t\n   104\t/-- Restatement of (1) in the direction most often used: SOT convergence gives pointwise\n   105\tconvergence. -/\n   106\ttheorem tendsto_sot_iff_forall_tendsto {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → SOT H} {A : SOT H} :\n   107\t    Tendsto T l (𝓝 A) ↔ ∀ x : H, Tendsto (fun i => (ofSOT (T i)) x) l (𝓝 ((ofSOT A) x)) :=\n   108\t  PointwiseConvergenceCLM.tendsto_iff_forall_tendsto\n   109\t\n   110\t/-- Strong limits are unique. -/\n   111\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.unique {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} [l.NeBot] {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   112\t    {A B : H →L[ℂ] H} (hA : StronglyTendsto l T A) (hB : StronglyTendsto l T B) : A = B := by\n   113\t  refine ContinuousLinearMap.ext fun x => ?_\n   114\t  exact tendsto_nhds_unique (hA x) (hB x)\n   115\t\n   116\ttheorem stronglyTendsto_const {ι : Type v} (l : Filter ι) (A : H →L[ℂ] H) :\n   117\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun _ => A) A := fun _ => tendsto_const_nhds\n   118\t\n   119\t/-- Norm convergence of operators implies strong convergence: the strong operator topology is\n   120\tcoarser than the operator-norm topology. -/\n   121\ttheorem stronglyTendsto_of_tendsto_norm {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   122\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : Tendsto T l (𝓝 A)) : StronglyTendsto l T A := fun x =>\n   123\t  ((ContinuousLinearMap.apply ℂ H x).continuous.tendsto _).comp hT\n   124\t\n   125\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.add {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   126\t    {A B : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) (hS : StronglyTendsto l S B) :\n   127\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => T i + S i) (A + B) := fun x => by\n   128\t  simpa using (hT x).add (hS x)\n   129\t\n   130\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.sub {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   131\t    {A B : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) (hS : StronglyTendsto l S B) :\n   132\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => T i - S i) (A - B) := fun x => by\n   133\t  simpa using (hT x).sub (hS x)\n   134\t\n   135\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.smul {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   136\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (c : ℂ) (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) :\n   137\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => c • T i) (c • A) := fun x => by\n   138\t  simpa using (hT x).const_smul c\n   139\t\n   140\t/-! ### Strongly closed sets of operators -/\n   141\t\n   142\t/-- A set of bounded operators is **strongly closed** when it is closed in the strong operator\n   143\ttopology. -/\n   144\tdef IsStronglyClosed (K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Prop :=\n   145\t  IsClosed {A : SOT H | ofSOT A ∈ K}\n   146\t\n   147\t/-- A strongly closed set contains the strong limit of any of its nets. -/\n   148\ttheorem IsStronglyClosed.mem_of_stronglyTendsto {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} (hK : IsStronglyClosed K)\n   149\t    {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} [l.NeBot] {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)} {A : H →L[ℂ] H}\n   150\t    (hmem : ∀ᶠ i in l, T i ∈ K) (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) : A ∈ K := by\n   151\t  have h : Tendsto (fun i => toSOT (T i)) l (𝓝 (toSOT A)) := stronglyTendsto_iff_tendsto_sot.1 hT\n   152\t  exact hK.mem_of_tendsto h (by simpa using hmem)\n   153\t\n   154\t/-- Conversely, a set closed under strong limits of nets is strongly closed.  (Stated with the\n   155\tcanonical neighbourhood filter net, which is the standard way to produce a converging net.) -/\n   156\ttheorem isStronglyClosed_of_mem_of_stronglyTendsto {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   157\t    (h : ∀ (l : Filter (SOT H)), l.NeBot → ∀ (A : H →L[ℂ] H),\n   158\t      (∀ᶠ B in l, ofSOT B ∈ K) → StronglyTendsto l (fun B => ofSOT B) A → A ∈ K) :\n   159\t    IsStronglyClosed K := by\n   160\t  rw [IsStronglyClosed, ← closure_subset_iff_isClosed]\n   161\t  intro A hA\n   162\t  set K' : Set (SOT H) := {B : SOT H | ofSOT B ∈ K} with hK'\n   163\t  have hne : (𝓝[K'] A).NeBot := mem_closure_iff_clusterPt.1 hA\n   164\t  have hmem : ∀ᶠ B in 𝓝[K'] A, ofSOT B ∈ K := self_mem_nhdsWithin\n   165\t  have htend : Tendsto (fun B : SOT H => B) (𝓝[K'] A) (𝓝 A) := nhdsWithin_le_nhds\n   166\t  have hconv : StronglyTendsto (𝓝[K'] A) (fun B : SOT H => ofSOT B) (ofSOT A) :=\n   167\t    stronglyTendsto_iff_tendsto_sot.2 (by simpa using htend)\n   168\t  exact h _ hne _ hmem hconv\n   169\t\n   170\t/-! ### (2) Commutants are strongly closed -/\n   171\t\n   172\t/-- **(2) Core conclusion (filter form).**  The commutant of an arbitrary set `S` of bounded\n   173\toperators is closed under strong limits. -/\n   174\ttheorem mem_centralizer_of_stronglyTendsto {S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι}\n   175\t    [l.NeBot] {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)} {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hmem : ∀ᶠ i in l, T i ∈ Set.centralizer S)\n   176\t    (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) : A ∈ Set.centralizer S := by\n   177\t  intro y hy\n   178\t  refine ContinuousLinearMap.ext fun x => ?_\n   179\t  have h1 : Tendsto (fun i => y (T i x)) l (𝓝 (y (A x))) := (y.continuous.tendsto _).comp (hT x)\n   180\t  have h2 : Tendsto (fun i => T i (y x)) l (𝓝 (A (y x))) := hT (y x)\n   181\t  have h3 : ∀ᶠ i in l, y (T i x) = T i (y x) := by\n   182\t    filter_upwards [hmem] with i hi\n   183\t    exact congrArg (fun t : H →L[ℂ] H => t x) (hi y hy)\n   184\t  have h1' : Tendsto (fun i => T i (y x)) l (𝓝 (y (A x))) := h1.congr' h3\n   185\t  have := tendsto_nhds_unique h2 h1'\n   186\t  simpa using this.symm\n   187\t\n   188\t/-- **(2) Core conclusion (topological form).**  The commutant of an arbitrary set of bounded\n   189\toperators is strongly closed. -/\n   190\ttheorem isStronglyClosed_centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   191\t    IsStronglyClosed (Set.centralizer S) := by\n   192\t  have hset : {A : SOT H | ofSOT A ∈ Set.centralizer S}\n   193\t      = ⋂ y ∈ S, ⋂ x : H, {A : SOT H | y ((ofSOT A) x) = (ofSOT A) (y x)} := by\n   194\t    ext A\n   195\t    simp only [Set.mem_setOf_eq, Set.mem_iInter]\n   196\t    constructor\n   197\t    · intro hA y hy x\n   198\t      have := congrArg (fun t : H →L[ℂ] H => t x) (hA y hy)\n   199\t      simpa using this\n   200\t    · intro h y hy\n   201\t      refine ContinuousLinearMap.ext fun x => ?_\n   202\t      simpa using h y hy x\n   203\t  rw [IsStronglyClosed, hset]\n   204\t  refine isClosed_biInter fun y _ => isClosed_iInter fun x => ?_\n   205\t  exact isClosed_eq (y.continuous.comp (continuous_eval_const x)) (continuous_eval_const (y x))\n   206\t\n   207\t/-! ### (3) Concrete von Neumann algebras are strongly closed -/\n   208\t\n   209\tsection VonNeumann\n   210\t\n   211\tvariable [CompleteSpace H]\n   212\t\n   213\t/-- The underlying set of a concrete von Neumann algebra is a commutant: this is Mathlib's\n   214\tdefining equality `M = M''`. -/\n   215\ttheorem coe_vonNeumannAlgebra_eq_centralizer (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) :\n   216\t    (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) = Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) :=\n   217\t  (M.centralizer_centralizer).symm\n   218\t\n   219\t/-- **(3) Core conclusion (filter form).**  A concrete von Neumann algebra contains the strong\n   220\tlimit of any of its nets. -/\n   221\ttheorem mem_vonNeumannAlgebra_of_stronglyTendsto (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) {ι : Type v}\n   222\t    {l : Filter ι} [l.NeBot] {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)} {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hmem : ∀ᶠ i in l, T i ∈ M)\n   223\t    (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) : A ∈ M := by\n   224\t  have hcoe : (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) = Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) :=\n   225\t    coe_vonNeumannAlgebra_eq_centralizer M\n   226\t  have hmem' : ∀ᶠ i in l, T i ∈ Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) := by\n   227\t    filter_upwards [hmem] with i hi\n   228\t    rw [← hcoe]\n   229\t    exact hi\n   230\t  have := mem_centralizer_of_stronglyTendsto hmem' hT\n   231\t  rw [← hcoe] at this\n   232\t  exact this\n   233\t\n   234\t/-- **(3) Core conclusion (topological form).**  Every concrete von Neumann algebra is strongly\n   235\tclosed. -/\n   236\ttheorem isStronglyClosed_vonNeumannAlgebra (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) :\n   237\t    IsStronglyClosed (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   238\t  rw [IsStronglyClosed, coe_vonNeumannAlgebra_eq_centralizer M]\n   239\t  exact isStronglyClosed_centralizer _\n   240\t\n   241\t/-- The same statement for the commutant of a von Neumann algebra. -/\n   242\ttheorem isStronglyClosed_commutant (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) :\n   243\t    IsStronglyClosed ((M.commutant : VonNeumannAlgebra H) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :=\n   244\t  isStronglyClosed_vonNeumannAlgebra _\n   245\t\n   246\t/-- Strong closedness of the bicommutant of a star subalgebra (the algebraic bicommutant of\n   247\t`AristotleCandidates.BicommutantVonNeumannCore`).  No identification of this bicommutant with a\n   248\tstrong closure is claimed. -/\n   249\ttheorem isStronglyClosed_vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   250\t    IsStronglyClosed ((StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated S : VonNeumannAlgebra H) :\n   251\t      Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :=\n   252\t  isStronglyClosed_vonNeumannAlgebra _\n   253\t\n   254\tend VonNeumann\n   255\t\n   256\t/-! ### (4) Multiplicative stability of strong limits -/\n   257\t\n   258\t/-- **(4a)** Left multiplication by a fixed bounded operator preserves strong limits. -/\n   259\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.const_mul {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   260\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) (B : H →L[ℂ] H) :\n   261\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => B * T i) (B * A) := by\n   262\t  intro x\n   263\t  simpa using (B.continuous.tendsto _).comp (hT x)\n   264\t\n   265\t/-- **(4b)** Right multiplication by a fixed bounded operator preserves strong limits. -/\n   266\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.mul_const {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   267\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) (B : H →L[ℂ] H) :\n   268\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => T i * B) (A * B) := by\n   269\t  intro x\n   270\t  simpa using hT (B x)\n   271\t\n   272\t/-- **(4c) Joint bounded-product corollary.**  If `T i → A` strongly with `‖T i‖` uniformly\n   273\tbounded, and `S i → B` strongly, then `T i * S i → A * B` strongly.  Uniform boundedness of the\n   274\tleft factor is what makes the product jointly strongly continuous. -/\n   275\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.mul_of_bounded {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   276\t    {A B : H →L[ℂ] H} {C : ℝ} (hC : ∀ i, ‖T i‖ ≤ C) (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A)\n   277\t    (hS : StronglyTendsto l S B) : StronglyTendsto l (fun i => T i * S i) (A * B) := by\n   278\t  intro x\n   279\t  have hgoal : Tendsto (fun i => ‖(T i * S i) x - (A * B) x‖) l (𝓝 0) := by\n   280\t    have ha : Tendsto (fun i => ‖S i x - B x‖) l (𝓝 0) :=\n   281\t      tendsto_iff_norm_sub_tendsto_zero.1 (hS x)\n   282\t    have hb : Tendsto (fun i => ‖T i (B x) - A (B x)‖) l (𝓝 0) :=\n   283\t      tendsto_iff_norm_sub_tendsto_zero.1 (hT (B x))\n   284\t    have hsum : Tendsto (fun i => C * ‖S i x - B x‖ + ‖T i (B x) - A (B x)‖) l (𝓝 0) := by\n   285\t      simpa using (ha.const_mul C).add hb\n   286\t    refine squeeze_zero (fun i => norm_nonneg _) (fun i => ?_) hsum\n   287\t    have hsplit : (T i * S i) x - (A * B) x\n   288\t        = T i (S i x - B x) + (T i (B x) - A (B x)) := by\n   289\t      simp [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply, map_sub]\n   290\t    have h1 : ‖T i (S i x - B x)‖ ≤ C * ‖S i x - B x‖ := by\n   291\t      refine le_trans ((T i).le_opNorm _) ?_\n   292\t      exact mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right (hC i) (norm_nonneg _)\n   293\t    calc ‖(T i * S i) x - (A * B) x‖\n   294\t        = ‖T i (S i x - B x) + (T i (B x) - A (B x))‖ := by rw [hsplit]\n   295\t      _ ≤ ‖T i (S i x - B x)‖ + ‖T i (B x) - A (B x)‖ := norm_add_le _ _\n   296\t      _ ≤ C * ‖S i x - B x‖ + ‖T i (B x) - A (B x)‖ := by linarith\n   297\t  exact tendsto_iff_norm_sub_tendsto_zero.2 hgoal\n   298\t\n   299\t/-- Variant of the joint bounded-product corollary with the uniform bound stated as an\n   300\texistential. -/\n   301\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.mul_of_norm_le {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   302\t    {A B : H →L[ℂ] H} (hbdd : ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ i, ‖T i‖ ≤ C) (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A)\n   303\t    (hS : StronglyTendsto l S B) : StronglyTendsto l (fun i => T i * S i) (A * B) := by\n   304\t  obtain ⟨C, hC⟩ := hbdd\n   305\t  exact hT.mul_of_bounded hC hS\n   306\t\n   307\t/-- A convenient special case of (4): compression of a uniformly bounded strong limit by two fixed\n   308\toperators, `B * T i * D → B * A * D` strongly. -/\n   309\ttheorem StronglyTendsto.compress {ι : Type v} {l : Filter ι} {T : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   310\t    {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hT : StronglyTendsto l T A) (B D : H →L[ℂ] H) :\n   311\t    StronglyTendsto l (fun i => B * T i * D) (B * A * D) :=\n   312\t  (hT.const_mul B).mul_const D\n   313\t\n   314\tend StrongOperator\n\n--- core definitions ---\n     1\timport Mathlib.Analysis.VonNeumannAlgebra.Basic\n     2\t\n     3\t/-!\n     4\t# The algebraic bicommutant of a star subalgebra of `B(H)`\n     5\t\n     6\tGiven a Hilbert space `H` and a star subalgebra `S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)` of the\n     7\tbounded operators on `H`, its *commutant* (centralizer) `S'` is again a star subalgebra, and\n     8\tthe double commutant `S''` satisfies `S'''' = S''`.  Hence `S''` is a *concrete* von Neumann\n     9\talgebra in the sense of `VonNeumannAlgebra H` (Mathlib's `structure` bundling a star\n    10\tsubalgebra which equals its own double commutant).\n    11\t\n    12\tThis file packages that construction as\n    13\t\n    14\t    StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H) → VonNeumannAlgebra H\n    15\t\n    16\tand proves the elementary order-theoretic facts which are available *purely* from the\n    17\tcentralizer API:\n    18\t\n    19\t* `StarSubalgebra.le_vonNeumannGenerated` — `S ≤ S''`;\n    20\t* `StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_mono` — `S ≤ T → S'' ≤ T''`;\n    21\t* `VonNeumannAlgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_toStarSubalgebra` — `M'' = M` for a concrete\n    22\t  von Neumann algebra `M`;\n    23\t* `StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_idem` — idempotence of the construction;\n    24\t* `StarSubalgebra.mem_vonNeumannGenerated_iff` — membership in `S''` is exactly\n    25\t  \"commutes with everything in the commutant of `S`\", and the variant\n    26\t  `StarSubalgebra.mem_vonNeumannGenerated_iff_commutant` phrased via\n    27\t  `VonNeumannAlgebra.commutant`.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Scope: algebra versus analysis\n    30\t\n    31\tEverything below is **purely algebraic**: `Set.centralizer` is used throughout and no\n    32\ttopology on `H →L[ℂ] H` enters.  In particular this file does **not** assert (and does not\n    33\tuse) the von Neumann *double commutant theorem*, i.e. the analytic statement that for a\n    34\tunital star subalgebra `S ⊆ B(H)` one has\n    35\t\n    36\t    S'' = closure of S in the strong operator topology\n    37\t        = closure of S in the weak operator topology.\n    38\t\n    39\tAt the pinned Mathlib commit that theorem is not available: there is no declaration in\n    40\t`Mathlib.Analysis.VonNeumannAlgebra.*` relating `Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer S)` to any\n    41\toperator-topology closure (the module docstring of `Mathlib/Analysis/VonNeumannAlgebra/Basic.lean`\n    42\texplicitly lists it as future work: *\"We'll also need to prove the von Neumann double commutant\n    43\ttheorem, that the concrete definition is equivalent to a *-closed subalgebra which is weakly\n    44\tclosed.\"*).  Accordingly no such statement is axiomatized here; only the algebraic facts that\n    45\tdo compile are stated.  Consequently `vonNeumannGenerated S` should be read as *the smallest\n    46\tconcrete von Neumann algebra containing `S` that the algebraic API can produce*, and its\n    47\tminimality among von Neumann algebras containing `S` is recorded below in\n    48\t`StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_le` (which *is* purely algebraic).\n    49\t-/\n    50\t\n    51\tuniverse u\n    52\t\n    53\tvariable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H]\n    54\t\n    55\tnamespace StarSubalgebra\n    56\t\n    57\t/-- The underlying set of the centralizer of a *star* subalgebra `S` of `H →L[ℂ] H` is just the\n    58\tset-theoretic centralizer of `S`: no star-closure is needed since `S` is already star closed. -/\n    59\ttheorem coe_centralizer_of_starSubalgebra (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    60\t    (StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) =\n    61\t      Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n    62\t  rw [StarSubalgebra.coe_centralizer, StarMemClass.star_coe_eq, Set.union_self]\n    63\t\n    64\t/-- The underlying set of the double centralizer of a star subalgebra `S` of `H →L[ℂ] H` is the\n    65\tset-theoretic double centralizer of `S`. -/\n    66\ttheorem coe_centralizer_centralizer_of_starSubalgebra (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    67\t    ((StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ\n    68\t        (StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    69\t          StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) =\n    70\t      Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) := by\n    71\t  rw [StarSubalgebra.coe_centralizer_centralizer, StarMemClass.star_coe_eq, Set.union_self]\n    72\t\n    73\t/-- The bicommutant of a star subalgebra `S` of the bounded operators on a Hilbert space `H`,\n    74\tpackaged as a concrete von Neumann algebra.\n    75\t\n    76\tThis is the **algebraic** double commutant construction; see the module docstring for the\n    77\trelation (not proved here) with the strong/weak operator closure. -/\n    78\tnoncomputable def vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    79\t    VonNeumannAlgebra H where\n    80\t  toStarSubalgebra :=\n    81\t    StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ\n    82\t      (StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))\n    83\t  centralizer_centralizer' := by\n    84\t    show Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer\n    85\t        ((StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ\n    86\t          (StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    87\t            StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) =\n    88\t      ((StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ\n    89\t          (StarSubalgebra.centralizer ℂ (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    90\t            StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))\n    91\t    rw [coe_centralizer_centralizer_of_starSubalgebra,\n    92\t      Set.centralizer_centralizer_centralizer]\n    93\t\n    94\t@[simp]\n    95\ttheorem coe_vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n    96\t    (vonNeumannGenerated S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) =\n    97\t      Set.centralizer (Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) := by\n    98\t  exact coe_centralizer_centralizer_of_starSubalgebra S\n    99\t\n   100\t/-- **Membership in the bicommutant**: `x ∈ S''` iff `x` commutes with every element of the\n   101\tcommutant of `S`. -/\n   102\ttheorem mem_vonNeumannGenerated_iff {S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)} {x : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n   103\t    x ∈ vonNeumannGenerated S ↔\n   104\t      ∀ y ∈ Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)), y * x = x * y := by\n   105\t  rw [← SetLike.mem_coe, coe_vonNeumannGenerated]\n   106\t  rfl\n   107\t\n   108\t/-- The commutant of `S''`, as a von Neumann algebra, has underlying set the commutant of `S`. -/\n   109\t@[simp]\n   110\ttheorem coe_commutant_vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   111\t    ((vonNeumannGenerated S).commutant : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) =\n   112\t      Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   113\t  rw [VonNeumannAlgebra.coe_commutant, coe_vonNeumannGenerated,\n   114\t    Set.centralizer_centralizer_centralizer]\n   115\t\n   116\t/-- Membership in the generated von Neumann algebra is characterized by commuting with the\n   117\tcommutant, phrased via `VonNeumannAlgebra.commutant`. -/\n   118\ttheorem mem_vonNeumannGenerated_iff_commutant {S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)}\n   119\t    {x : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n   120\t    x ∈ vonNeumannGenerated S ↔ ∀ y ∈ (vonNeumannGenerated S).commutant, y * x = x * y := by\n   121\t  rw [mem_vonNeumannGenerated_iff]\n   122\t  refine forall_congr' fun y => ?_\n   123\t  rw [← SetLike.mem_coe, coe_commutant_vonNeumannGenerated]\n   124\t\n   125\t/-- **(1)** A star subalgebra is contained in its bicommutant. -/\n   126\ttheorem le_vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   127\t    (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) ⊆ (vonNeumannGenerated S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   128\t  rw [coe_vonNeumannGenerated]\n   129\t  exact Set.subset_centralizer_centralizer\n   130\t\n   131\ttheorem mem_vonNeumannGenerated_of_mem {S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)} {x : H →L[ℂ] H}\n   132\t    (hx : x ∈ S) : x ∈ vonNeumannGenerated S :=\n   133\t  le_vonNeumannGenerated S hx\n   134\t\n   135\t/-- **(2)** The bicommutant construction is monotone. -/\n   136\ttheorem vonNeumannGenerated_mono {S T : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)} (h : S ≤ T) :\n   137\t    vonNeumannGenerated S ≤ vonNeumannGenerated T := by\n   138\t  intro x hx\n   139\t  rw [← SetLike.mem_coe, coe_vonNeumannGenerated] at hx ⊢\n   140\t  exact Set.centralizer_subset (Set.centralizer_subset h) hx\n   141\t\n   142\tend StarSubalgebra\n   143\t\n   144\tnamespace VonNeumannAlgebra\n   145\t\n   146\topen StarSubalgebra\n   147\t\n   148\t/-- **(3)** The bicommutant of (the star subalgebra underlying) a concrete von Neumann algebra\n   149\tis that von Neumann algebra itself. -/\n   150\t@[simp]\n   151\ttheorem vonNeumannGenerated_toStarSubalgebra (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) :\n   152\t    vonNeumannGenerated M.toStarSubalgebra = M := by\n   153\t  refine SetLike.coe_injective ?_\n   154\t  rw [coe_vonNeumannGenerated]\n   155\t  exact M.centralizer_centralizer\n   156\t\n   157\tend VonNeumannAlgebra\n   158\t\n   159\tnamespace StarSubalgebra\n   160\t\n   161\t/-- **(4)** The construction is idempotent at the `VonNeumannAlgebra` level. -/\n   162\t@[simp]\n   163\ttheorem vonNeumannGenerated_idem (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   164\t    vonNeumannGenerated (vonNeumannGenerated S).toStarSubalgebra = vonNeumannGenerated S :=\n   165\t  VonNeumannAlgebra.vonNeumannGenerated_toStarSubalgebra _\n   166\t\n   167\t/-- Minimality: `S''` is contained in any concrete von Neumann algebra containing `S`.\n   168\tThis is purely algebraic (no operator-topology closure is involved). -/\n   169\ttheorem vonNeumannGenerated_le {S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)} {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H}\n   170\t    (h : (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) ⊆ (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H))) :\n   171\t    (vonNeumannGenerated S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) ⊆ (M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   172\t  rw [coe_vonNeumannGenerated, ← M.centralizer_centralizer]\n   173\t  exact Set.centralizer_subset (Set.centralizer_subset h)\n   174\t\n   175\t/-- The commutant of `S` and the commutant of `S''` agree (as sets of operators):\n   176\ttaking commutants three times is the same as taking it once. -/\n   177\ttheorem centralizer_vonNeumannGenerated (S : StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)) :\n   178\t    Set.centralizer (vonNeumannGenerated S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) =\n   179\t      Set.centralizer (S : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) := by\n   180\t  rw [coe_vonNeumannGenerated, Set.centralizer_centralizer_centralizer]\n   181\t\n   182\tend StarSubalgebra\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba sources/manuscript/main.tex | sed -n '1260,1295p' && printf '\\\\n--- Git diff (candidate only) ---\\\\n' && git diff --no-index /dev/null AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean | sed -n '1,220p'; test \"'${PIPESTATUS[0]} -eq 0 || true && printf '\"'\\\\n--- tracked/ignored response status ---\\\\n' && git check-ignore -v aristotle_responses/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.tar.gz aristotle_responses/P49B-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49b-sn5_fvfw_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean || true && git diff --check\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba sources/manuscript/main.tex | sed -n '1260,1295p' && printf '\\\\n--- Git diff (candidate only) ---\\\\n' && git diff --no-index /dev/null AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean | sed -n '1,220p'; test \"'${PIPESTATUS[0]} -eq 0 || true && printf '\"'\\\\n--- tracked/ignored response status ---\\\\n' && git check-ignore -v aristotle_responses/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.tar.gz aristotle_responses/P49B-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49b-sn5_fvfw_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean || true && git diff --check\"","aggregated_output":"  1260\t\\end{equation}\n  1261\t\\end{proof}\n  1262\tNow that we know that the state is tracial on the compression of the\n  1263\t$C^\\ast$-algebra, we want to extend this result to the full von Neumann\n  1264\talgebra $M_\\G$.\n  1265\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:normal-trace}\n  1266\tThe state $\\tau_q$ defines a normal tracial state on $q\\cM_\\Gamma q$.\n  1267\t\\end{proposition}\n  1268\t\n  1269\t\\begin{proof}\n  1270\tBy Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists} $q \\in M_\\G$ and \\cite[I.9.1.4]{Blackadar} implies\n  1271\tthat $qM_\\G q$ is a von\n  1272\tNeumann algebra considered on the Hilbert space $q \\cH_\\w$.\n  1273\tTo proof the assertion, we need to first proof, that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly\n  1274\tdense in $q M_\\G q$, to approximate the functional on it.\n  1275\tFor that, let $x \\in M_\\G$. By Kaplanskys Density theorem\n  1276\t\\cite[I.9.1.3]{Blackadar}, there exist a uniformly bounded net $(x_i)_{i\\in I}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ with $x_i \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} x$ and hence\n  1277\tstrongly. By the same argument as in the previous lemma then\n  1278\t\\begin{equation}\n  1279\t    q x_i q \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} q x q,\n  1280\t\\end{equation}\n  1281\tstrongly which proves that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly dense in $q M_\\G q$. The\n  1282\tsame argument implies that for every $a,b \\in M_\\G$ there exist uniformly bounded nets $(a_i)_{i\\in I},(b_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ such that\n  1283\t\\begin{equation}\n  1284\t   q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q,\n  1285\t   \\qquad\n  1286\t   q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q.\n  1287\t\\end{equation}\n  1288\tTo argue for the convergence of the product, we can introduce a joint index $k$\\footnote{For example $k\n  1289\t    = (i,j)$ so that $a_{(i,j)} = a_i, b_{(i,j)} = b_j$ and $(i',j') \\preceq (i,j)\n  1290\t    \\Leftrightarrow i' \\preceq i \\land j' \\preceq j$},\n  1291\tso that by the same argument as in the proof of Lemma \\ref{lem:finite-trace}\n  1292\t\\begin{equation}\n  1293\t    q a_i q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q b q,\n  1294\t    \\qquad\n  1295\t    q b_j q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q a q.\n\n--- Git diff (candidate only) ---\ndiff --git a/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean b/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..09bfcf6\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean\n@@ -0,0 +1,491 @@\n+import Mathlib\n+import AristotleCandidates.BicommutantVonNeumannCore\n+import AristotleCandidates.StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann\n+\n+/-!\n+# P49B — the analytic von Neumann bicommutant (double commutant) theorem\n+\n+**Infrastructure only.**  This file makes no manuscript claim.  It proves, for an arbitrary\n+complex Hilbert space `H` and an arbitrary star subalgebra `A ⊆ B(H)`, the *analytic* double\n+commutant theorem\n+\n+    strongClosure A = A''  ( = `StarSubalgebra.vonNeumannGenerated A` ),\n+\n+where `strongClosure` is the closure in the **strong operator topology** and `A''` is the\n+algebraic bicommutant of `AristotleCandidates.BicommutantVonNeumannCore`.\n+\n+## Plan alignment\n+\n+* **Package and lane:** `P49B`, lane `shared` (analytic infrastructure used by Lane G).\n+* **Predecessors:** `P49A` (`AristotleCandidates.StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann`) and the\n+  algebraic bicommutant core (`AristotleCandidates.BicommutantVonNeumannCore`), both imported.\n+* **Gate discharged:** the bicommutant of a represented unital complex star algebra equals its\n+  strong-operator closure.\n+* **Forbidden downstream claims, not made here:** no Kaplansky unit-ball density, no corner\n+  trace, no normality or faithfulness statement, no finiteness or fullness, and no factor\n+  classification.\n+\n+## Hypotheses actually used\n+\n+* `H` is a complex inner product space which is **complete** (`[CompleteSpace H]`).  Completeness\n+  is genuinely used: the cyclic subspace has to carry an orthogonal projection, and adjoints of\n+  bounded operators are needed for the invariant-subspace argument.\n+* `A` is a `StarSubalgebra ℂ (H →L[ℂ] H)`.  In Mathlib a `StarSubalgebra` over `ℂ` is in\n+  particular a unital subalgebra, so the **unitality** hypothesis `1 ∈ A` of the classical\n+  statement is automatic and is recorded by `StarSubalgebra.one_mem`; it is used exactly once, to\n+  see that the cyclic vector `ξ` lies in the cyclic subspace generated by `A`.  No further\n+  nondegeneracy assumption is required.\n+\n+No structure field, external token, axiom, `sorry`, `opaque` or `implemented_by` is used: the\n+central equality `strongClosure A = A''` is proved.\n+\n+## Method\n+\n+The nontrivial inclusion `A'' ⊆ strongClosure A` is the standard **finite amplification**\n+argument, developed here from scratch because the pinned Mathlib does not contain it:\n+\n+1. `VonNeumannBicommutant.ampOp` — the diagonal amplification of an operator to\n+   `Amp H n = PiLp 2 (fun _ : Fin n => H)`, assembled into a star algebra homomorphism\n+   `VonNeumannBicommutant.ampHom`;\n+2. `VonNeumannBicommutant.commute_starProjection_of_invariant` — if a closed subspace is\n+   invariant under `b` and under `star b`, the orthogonal projection onto it commutes with `b`;\n+3. `VonNeumannBicommutant.ampOp_commute_of_mem_bicommutant` — the matrix-entry computation showing\n+   that `T ∈ A''` implies `ampOp n T ∈ (ampOp n '' A)''`;\n+4. `VonNeumannBicommutant.exists_mem_approx_on_fin` — the resulting simultaneous approximation on\n+   an *arbitrary finite family* of vectors.\n+\n+The strong closure is defined as a genuine topological closure in Mathlib's\n+`PointwiseConvergenceCLM` (= the strong operator topology, as used by `P49A`), so the theorem is a\n+statement about arbitrary finite strong neighbourhoods / nets, not about sequences;\n+`VonNeumannBicommutant.mem_strongClosure_iff` makes the finite-neighbourhood form explicit.\n+-/\n+\n+universe u\n+\n+open Filter Topology ContinuousLinearMap\n+\n+namespace VonNeumannBicommutant\n+\n+/-! ## The strong operator closure of a set of operators -/\n+\n+section StrongClosure\n+\n+variable {H : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H]\n+\n+/-- The **strong operator closure** of a set `K` of bounded operators: the closure of `K` inside\n+`StrongOperator.SOT H`, Mathlib's strong-operator-topologized copy of `H →L[ℂ] H`. -/\n+def strongClosure (K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : Set (H →L[ℂ] H) :=\n+  {T | StrongOperator.toSOT T ∈\n+    closure {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ K}}\n+\n+theorem subset_strongClosure (K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)) : K ⊆ strongClosure K := fun _ hT =>\n+  @subset_closure (StrongOperator.SOT H) _ _ _ hT\n+\n+/-- The strong closure is contained in any strongly closed set containing `K`.  (`P49A`'s\n+`StrongOperator.IsStronglyClosed` is used as the notion of strong closedness.) -/\n+theorem strongClosure_subset {K C : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} (hKC : K ⊆ C)\n+    (hC : StrongOperator.IsStronglyClosed C) : strongClosure K ⊆ C := by\n+  intro T hT\n+  have h : closure {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ K}\n+      ⊆ {B : StrongOperator.SOT H | StrongOperator.ofSOT B ∈ C} :=\n+    closure_minimal (fun B hB => hKC hB) hC\n+  exact h hT\n+\n+/-- **Finite-neighbourhood form, sufficiency.**  If `T` can be approximated by elements of `K`\n+simultaneously on every finite set of vectors, then `T` lies in the strong closure of `K`. -/\n+theorem mem_strongClosure_of_approx {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n+    (h : ∀ (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε → ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε) :\n+    T ∈ strongClosure K := by\n+  classical\n+  rw [strongClosure, Set.mem_setOf_eq, mem_closure_iff_nhds]\n+  intro U hU\n+  have hind := PointwiseConvergenceCLM.isEmbedding_coeFn (σ := RingHom.id ℂ) (E := H) (F := H)\n+  rw [hind.isInducing.nhds_eq_comap, Filter.mem_comap] at hU\n+  obtain ⟨V, hV, hVU⟩ := hU\n+  rw [nhds_pi, Filter.mem_pi] at hV\n+  obtain ⟨I, hI, t, ht, htV⟩ := hV\n+  have hr : ∀ x : H, ∃ r : ℝ, 0 < r ∧ Metric.ball (T x) r ⊆ t x := fun x =>\n+    Metric.mem_nhds_iff.1 (ht x)\n+  choose r hr0 hrt using hr\n+  set s : Finset H := hI.toFinset with hs\n+  set ε : ℝ := (insert (1 : ℝ) (s.image r)).min' (by simp) with hε\n+  have hε0 : 0 < ε := by\n+    have hmem := Finset.min'_mem (insert (1 : ℝ) (s.image r)) (by simp)\n+    rcases Finset.mem_insert.1 hmem with h1 | h1\n+    · rw [hε, h1]; norm_num\n+    · obtain ⟨x, _, hx⟩ := Finset.mem_image.1 h1\n+      rw [hε, ← hx]; exact hr0 x\n+  have hεle : ∀ x ∈ s, ε ≤ r x := fun x hx =>\n+    Finset.min'_le _ _ (Finset.mem_insert_of_mem (Finset.mem_image_of_mem r hx))\n+  obtain ⟨a, haK, ha⟩ := h s ε hε0\n+  refine ⟨StrongOperator.toSOT a, hVU (htV ?_), haK⟩\n+  intro x hx\n+  have hxs : x ∈ s := by rw [hs]; simpa using hx\n+  refine hrt x ?_\n+  rw [Metric.mem_ball, dist_eq_norm]\n+  calc ‖a x - T x‖ = ‖T x - a x‖ := by rw [norm_sub_rev]\n+    _ < ε := ha x hxs\n+    _ ≤ r x := hεle x hxs\n+\n+/-- **Finite-neighbourhood form, necessity.**  An element of the strong closure of `K` is\n+approximated by elements of `K` simultaneously on every finite set of vectors. -/\n+theorem approx_of_mem_strongClosure {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H}\n+    (hT : T ∈ strongClosure K) (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) :\n+    ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε := by\n+  classical\n+  rw [strongClosure, Set.mem_setOf_eq, mem_closure_iff_nhds] at hT\n+  have hind := PointwiseConvergenceCLM.isEmbedding_coeFn (σ := RingHom.id ℂ) (E := H) (F := H)\n+  set V : Set (H → H) := Set.pi (F : Set H) (fun x => Metric.ball (T x) ε) with hV\n+  have hVnhds : V ∈ 𝓝 (fun x => T x : H → H) := by\n+    rw [nhds_pi]\n+    exact Filter.pi_mem_pi F.finite_toSet (fun x _ => Metric.ball_mem_nhds _ hε)\n+  set U : Set (StrongOperator.SOT H) := (fun B : StrongOperator.SOT H => (B : H → H)) ⁻¹' V\n+    with hU\n+  have hUnhds : U ∈ 𝓝 (StrongOperator.toSOT T) := by\n+    rw [hind.isInducing.nhds_eq_comap]\n+    exact Filter.mem_comap.2 ⟨V, hVnhds, subset_rfl⟩\n+  obtain ⟨B, hBU, hBK⟩ := hT U hUnhds\n+  refine ⟨StrongOperator.ofSOT B, hBK, fun x hx => ?_⟩\n+  have h2 := hBU x hx\n+  rw [Metric.mem_ball, dist_eq_norm] at h2\n+  rw [norm_sub_rev]\n+  exact h2\n+\n+/-- **Membership in the strong closure is exactly simultaneous finite approximation.**  This is\n+the \"arbitrary finite strong neighbourhood\" form of the closure. -/\n+theorem mem_strongClosure_iff {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n+    T ∈ strongClosure K ↔\n+      ∀ (F : Finset H) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε → ∃ a ∈ K, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - a x‖ < ε :=\n+  ⟨fun hT F ε hε => approx_of_mem_strongClosure hT F ε hε, mem_strongClosure_of_approx⟩\n+\n+/-- Net form: the strong closure contains every strong limit of a net taken in `K`. -/\n+theorem mem_strongClosure_of_stronglyTendsto {K : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {ι : Type*} {l : Filter ι}\n+    [l.NeBot] {S : ι → (H →L[ℂ] H)} {T : H →L[ℂ] H} (hmem : ∀ᶠ i in l, S i ∈ K)\n+    (hS : StrongOperator.StronglyTendsto l S T) : T ∈ strongClosure K := by\n+  refine mem_strongClosure_of_approx fun F ε hε => ?_\n+  have hall : ∀ᶠ i in l, ∀ x ∈ F, ‖T x - S i x‖ < ε := by\n+    refine (Filter.eventually_all_finset F).2 fun x _ => ?_\n+    have h0 : Tendsto (fun i => ‖T x - S i x‖) l (𝓝 0) := by\n+      have := (hS x).const_sub (T x)\n+      simpa using this.norm\n+    exact h0.eventually (eventually_lt_nhds hε)\n+  obtain ⟨i, hiK, hi⟩ := (hmem.and hall).exists\n+  exact ⟨S i, hiK, hi⟩\n+\n+end StrongClosure\n+\n+/-! ## Invariant subspaces and orthogonal projections -/\n+\n+section Invariant\n+\n+variable {E : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℂ E] [CompleteSpace E]\n+\n+/-- If a closed subspace `K` is invariant under `b` and under `star b`, then the orthogonal\n+projection onto `K` commutes with `b`. -/\n+theorem commute_starProjection_of_invariant (K : Submodule ℂ E) [K.HasOrthogonalProjection]\n+    (b : E →L[ℂ] E) (h1 : ∀ x ∈ K, b x ∈ K) (h2 : ∀ x ∈ K, (star b) x ∈ K) :\n+    b * K.starProjection = K.starProjection * b := by\n+  have horth : ∀ y ∈ Kᗮ, b y ∈ Kᗮ := by\n+    intro y hy\n+    rw [Submodule.mem_orthogonal]\n+    intro z hz\n+    have h : inner ℂ z (b y) = inner ℂ ((star b) z) y := by\n+      rw [ContinuousLinearMap.star_eq_adjoint, ContinuousLinearMap.adjoint_inner_left]\n+    rw [h]\n+    exact (Submodule.mem_orthogonal K y).1 hy _ (h2 z hz)\n+  ext x\n+  simp only [ContinuousLinearMap.mul_apply]\n+  have h3 : b (K.starProjection x) ∈ K := h1 _ (Submodule.starProjection_apply_mem K x)\n+  have h4 : b (x - K.starProjection x) ∈ Kᗮ :=\n+    horth _ (Submodule.sub_starProjection_mem_orthogonal x)\n+  have key : K.starProjection (b x)\n+      = K.starProjection (b (K.starProjection x))\n+        + K.starProjection (b (x - K.starProjection x)) := by\n+    rw [← map_add, ← map_add]\n+    congr 1\n+    abel_nf\n+  rw [key, Submodule.starProjection_eq_self_iff.2 h3,\n+    (Submodule.starProjection_apply_eq_zero_iff K).mpr h4, add_zero]\n+\n+end Invariant\n+\n+/-! ## Finite amplifications -/\n+\n+section Amplification\n\n--- tracked/ignored response status ---\n.gitignore:3:aristotle_responses/\taristotle_responses/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem.tar.gz\n.gitignore:3:aristotle_responses/\taristotle_responses/P49B-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49b-sn5_fvfw_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
